ter","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/binary-protocol/cluster","content":"The replica-to-replica plane: its dedicated TCP port, command discriminants, and traffic that never reaches a client."},"169":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/cluster-1","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/cluster","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/binary-protocol/cluster#transport","content":"Transport"},"170":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/cluster-2","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/cluster","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/binary-protocol/cluster#command-discriminants","content":"Command discriminants"},"171":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/cluster-3","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/cluster","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/binary-protocol/cluster#frame-integrity","content":"Frame integrity"},"172":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/cluster-4","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/cluster","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/binary-protocol/cluster#handshake","content":"Handshake"},"173":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/cluster-5","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/cluster","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/binary-protocol/cluster#replication","content":"Replication"},"174":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/cluster-6","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/cluster","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/binary-protocol/cluster#prepare-6","content":"Prepare (6)"},"175":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/cluster-7","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/cluster","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/binary-protocol/cluster#prepareok-7","content":"PrepareOk (7)"},"176":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/cluster-8","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/cluster","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/binary-protocol/cluster#commit-9","content":"Commit (9)"},"177":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/cluster-9","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/cluster","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/binary-protocol/cluster#view-changes","content":"View changes"},"178":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/cluster-10","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/cluster","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/binary-protocol/cluster#journal-repair","content":"Journal repair"},"179":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/cluster-11","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/cluster","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/binary-protocol/cluster#state-transfer","content":"State transfer"},"180":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/cluster-12","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/cluster","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/binary-protocol/cluster#session-forwarding","content":"Session forwarding"},"181":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/cluster-13","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/cluster","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/binary-protocol/cluster#consensus-planes","content":"Consensus planes"},"182":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/cluster-14","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/cluster","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/binary-protocol/cluster","content":"Replicas talk to each other with the same 256-byte framing the client protocol uses, on a dedicated TCP port, with their own set of command discriminants. None of these frames ever appears on a client connection, and a client cannot reach this plane: it is a separate listener, gated by a handshake."},"183":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/cluster-15","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/cluster","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/binary-protocol/cluster","content":"This page documents the replica plane as of binary protocol 0.11.0. It is internal protocol: only Iggy servers speak it, and it can change between server releases without a client-facing version bump."},"184":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/cluster-16","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/cluster","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/binary-protocol/cluster#transport","content":"Every node in [[cluster.nodes]] exposes a tcp_replica port next to the client ports: ports = { tcp = 8090, quic = 8080, http = 3000, websocket = 8092, tcp_replica = 9090 }. The node's ip is the roster address for the replica plane (clients fall back to it when advertised_address is unset)."},"185":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/cluster-17","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/cluster","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/binary-protocol/cluster#transport","content":"The replica plane is TCP only, by design: the prepare hash chain, cross-shard fd delegation, and view-change timing all assume an ordered byte stream."},"186":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/cluster-18","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/cluster","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/binary-protocol/cluster#transport","content":"Directional dialing: a replica dials only peers with a strictly greater replica_id and accepts inbound connections only from strictly lower ids. Exactly one connection exists per pair, with no tiebreaker races."},"187":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/cluster-19","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/cluster","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/binary-protocol/cluster#transport","content":"Frames are the standard [256-byte header][optional body] with size at offset 48. The maximum frame is 64 MiB by default (message_bus.max_message_size). Headers are #[repr(C)], decoded zero-copy, and require 16-byte alignment."},"188":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/cluster-20","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/cluster","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/binary-protocol/cluster#transport","content":"Nothing inside a frame marks it as replica traffic. The separation is structural: client listeners parse every inbound frame as a RequestHeader (command 5 only), the replica listener requires the first frame to be ReplicaHello, and the client-bound commands Reply (8) and Eviction (13) are rejected if they arrive on the replica plane."},"189":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/cluster-21","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/cluster","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/binary-protocol/cluster#command-discriminants","content":"The full command byte registry, client values included. Values above 29 are rejected."},"190":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/cluster-22","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/cluster"