:"text","url":"/docs/binary-protocol/cluster#command-discriminants","content":"ForwardLogoutResult"},"300":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/cluster-132","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/cluster","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/binary-protocol/cluster#command-discriminants","content":"primary to backup"},"301":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/cluster-133","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/cluster","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/binary-protocol/cluster#command-discriminants","content":"Logout outcome"},"302":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/cluster-134","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/cluster","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/binary-protocol/cluster#command-discriminants","content":"There is no dedicated replica heartbeat: liveness is the Commit broadcast, sent on cluster.commit_broadcast_interval."},"303":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/cluster-135","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/cluster","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/binary-protocol/cluster#frame-integrity","content":"The checksum and checksum_body fields that client frames leave zero are live on the replica plane:"},"304":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/cluster-136","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/cluster","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/binary-protocol/cluster#frame-integrity","content":"Frame seal (checksum, bytes 0..16): XxHash3-64 over header bytes 16..256, widened to u128. Sealed on every replica message except Prepare, RepairPrepare, and the three handshake frames (whose integrity comes from the keyed MAC). Verified once, at typed decode, before any field validation; a mismatch drops the frame."},"305":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/cluster-137","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/cluster","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/binary-protocol/cluster#frame-integrity","content":"Prepare identity (Prepare / RepairPrepare only): those two spend checksum on an identity hash instead - XxHash3-64 over the whole 256-byte header computed with checksum = 0 and view = 0, so a retransmit that re-stamps view keeps the same identity. PrepareOk echoes it in prepare_checksum, and each prepare's parent field carries the previous prepare's identity, forming a hash chain."},"306":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/cluster-138","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/cluster","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/binary-protocol/cluster#frame-integrity","content":"Body seal (checksum_body, bytes 16..32): three regimes. Metadata-plane prepares seal their body with XxHash3-64. Partition-plane prepares leave it zero: the message batch inside already carries batch_checksum, verified at network ingress. DoViewChange and StartView seal their suffix bodies. Every other message leaves it zero (state-transfer bodies are verified per artifact, not per frame)."},"307":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/cluster-139","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/cluster","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/binary-protocol/cluster#frame-integrity","content":"The seals are unkeyed integrity checks, not authentication. Peer authentication comes from the handshake below; without cluster.auth and TLS enabled, the replica port trusts any peer that can reach it. Do not expose tcp_replica beyond the cluster network."},"308":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/cluster-140","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/cluster","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/binary-protocol/cluster#frame-integrity","content":"Prepare, RepairPrepare, and the session-forwarding headers validate their reserved regions as zero; the other replica headers leave reserved bytes covered by the frame seal only."},"309":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/cluster-141","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/cluster","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/binary-protocol/cluster#handshake","content":"Commands 14-16, exchanged before any consensus traffic. These are raw GenericHeader frames (size = 256) using the per-command area at bytes 128..256:"},"310":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/cluster-142","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/cluster","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/binary-protocol/cluster#handshake","content":"Offset"},"311":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/cluster-143","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/cluster","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/binary-protocol/cluster#handshake","content":"Size"},"312":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/cluster-144","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/cluster","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/binary-protocol/cluster#handshake","content":"Content"},"313":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/cluster-145","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/cluster","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/binary-protocol/cluster#handshake","content":"128"},"314":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/cluster-146","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/cluster","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/binary-protocol/cluster#handshake","content":"32"},"315":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/cluster-147","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/cluster","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/binary-protocol/cluster#handshake","content":"Nonce (dialer's in Hello, acceptor's in Challenge)"},"316":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/cluster-148","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/cluster","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/binary-protocol/cluster#handshake","content":"160"},"317":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/cluster-149","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/cluster","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/binary-protocol/cluster#handshake","content":"32"},"318":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/cluster-150","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/cluster","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/binary-protocol/cluster#handshake","content":"BLAKE3 keyed MAC (acceptor's in Challenge, dialer's in Finish)"},"319":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/cluster-151","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/cluster","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/binary-protocol/cluster#handshake","content":"192"},"320":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/cluster-152","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/cluster","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/binary-protocol/cluster#handshake","content":"1"},"321":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/cluster-153","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/cluster","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/binary-protocol/cluster#handshake","content":"Challenge only: handshake status"},"322":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/cluster-154","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/cluster","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/binary-protocol/cluster#handshake","content":"Statuses: 0 Ok, 1 UnknownCommand, 2 ClusterMismatch, 3 DirectionalRule; values 4 and 5 are reserved (never sent), and a dialer treats any unknown status byte as a rejection. The MAC key derives from cluster.auth.shared_secret (32+ bytes; previous_shared_secret gives a rotation window), and with cluster TLS enabled (TLS 1.3 only, ALPN iggy-replica) the TLS exporter is folded into every MAC as channel binding. The handshake authenticates cluster membership, not per-replica identity: one shared secret for the whole cluster."},"323":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/cluster-155","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/cluster","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/binary-protocol/cluster#handshake","content":"The cluster header field (bytes 32..48) is the first 16 bytes of blake3(cluster_name) as a little-endian u128 on every replica frame, checked during the handshake, so nodes from a differently named cluster cannot connect even with auth disabled."},"324":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/cluster-156","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/cluster","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/binary-protocol/cluster#prepare-6","content":"Per-command fields (bytes 128..256):"},"325":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/cluster-157","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/cluster","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/binary-protocol/cluster#prepare-6","content":"Offset"},"326":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/cluster-158","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/cluster","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/binary-protocol/cluster#prepare-6","content":"Size"},"327":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/cluster-159","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/cluster","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/binary-protocol/cluster#prepare-6","