e-6","content":"28"},"371":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/cluster-203","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/cluster","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/binary-protocol/cluster#prepare-6","content":"reserved"},"372":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/cluster-204","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/cluster","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/binary-protocol/cluster#prepare-6","content":"Zero"},"373":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/cluster-205","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/cluster","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/binary-protocol/cluster#prepare-6","content":"For metadata operations the body is the admitted command's payload verbatim. For SendMessages it is the message batch as stamped by the primary at journal append (base_offset, base_timestamp, and the recomputed batch_checksum are final); each backup re-derives the expected stamp from its own position and refuses a mismatch. Replication is chain-form: the primary sends to (replica + 1) % replica_count, each backup forwards to its own successor, and the chain stops when the next hop is the primary. RepairPrepare (19) is byte-identical to a journaled prepare with only the command byte rewritten."},"374":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/cluster-206","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/cluster","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/binary-protocol/cluster#prepareok-7","content":"Header-only. parent (echo) at 128, prepare_checksum (echo of the prepare's identity) at 144, then op 160, commit 168, timestamp 176, request 184, operation 192, group 200. Two fields are the acker's own, not echoes: header view is the acking replica's view, and commit is its own commit point. Sent to the view's primary."},"375":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/cluster-207","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/cluster","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/binary-protocol/cluster#commit-9","content":"Header-only broadcast; validation rejects size != 256. timestamp_monotonic at 144 is the liveness token (a receiver resets its heartbeat timer only on a strictly newer value), commit at 152 is the commit point, group at 168. Bytes 128..144 and 160..168 are declared (commit_checksum, checkpoint_op) but currently unused."},"376":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/cluster-208","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/cluster","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/binary-protocol/cluster#view-changes","content":"StartViewChange (10): header-only, just group at 128. Broadcast when a replica suspects the primary."},"377":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/cluster-209","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/cluster","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/binary-protocol/cluster#view-changes","content":"DoViewChange (11): op (highest op) 128, commit 136, group 144, log_view 152 (view when the sender was last in Normal status), nack_bitset 224 (bit i = \"I never prepared suffix entry i\"; truncation authority), present_bitset 240 (bit i = \"I can serve entry i's body\"). Body = the log suffix as up to 128 packed 256-byte PrepareHeaders, headers only. Broadcast."},"378":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/cluster-210","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/cluster","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/binary-protocol/cluster#view-changes","content":"StartView (12): op 128, commit 136 (max over the DVC quorum), group 144, incarnation 240 (echo of a RequestStartView incarnation, zero when unsolicited). Body = the view's suffix as packed PrepareHeaders, highest op first; empty is legal. Unicast when answering a probe, broadcast otherwise."},"379":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/cluster-211","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/cluster","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/binary-protocol/cluster#view-changes","content":"RequestStartView (17): header-only recovery probe with group 128 and incarnation 240. A restarted replica broadcasts it instead of trusting stale local state; only the current view's primary answers, with a targeted StartView."},"380":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/cluster-212","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/cluster","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/binary-protocol/cluster#journal-repair","content":"A replica missing committed prepares (rejoin window or interior hole) pulls them from a Normal-status peer (the metadata plane also serves repair during a view change, so a forming primary cannot deadlock):"},"381":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/cluster-213","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/cluster","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/binary-protocol/cluster#journal-repair","content":"RequestPrepares (18): nonce 128, from_op 144, to_op 152, group 160. from_op = 0 or from_op > to_op is rejected."},"382":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/cluster-214","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/cluster","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/binary-protocol/cluster#journal-repair","content":"RepairPrepare (19): the journaled prepares, streamed in op order."},"383":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/cluster-215","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/cluster","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/binary-protocol/cluster#journal-repair","content":"RepairDone (20) / RangeEvicted (21): one shared layout (nonce 128, op 144, group 152) under two command bytes. RepairDone.op = last op served; RangeEvicted.op = oldest op the peer still retains, the honest answer when the front of the range is gone."},"384":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/cluster-216","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/cluster","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/binary-protocol/cluster#journal-repair","content":"Pacing is bounded by cluster.repair_chunk_max, which must stay below message_bus.peer_queue_capacity: per-peer send queues drop overruns silently."},"385":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/cluster-217","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/cluster","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/binary-protocol/cluster#state-transfer","content":"Bulk catch-up for a replica too far behind for journal repair. Pull-based and lockstep (at most one chunk in flight per artifact), because per-peer queues drop overruns:"},"386":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/cluster-218","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/cluster","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/binary-protocol/cluster#state-transfer","content":"RequestStateTransfer (22): nonce 128, group 144."},"387":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/cluster-219","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/cluster","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/binary-protocol/cluster#state-transfer","content":"StateTransferTarget (23): nonce 128, commit_op 144, group 152, available 160, unavailable_transient 161, commit_max 168. available = 0 is a header-only refusal. Body = the state manifest."},"388":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/cluster-220","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/cluster","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/binary-protocol/cluster#state-transfer","content":"RequestStateChunk (24): nonce 128, offset 144, group 152, len 160, artifact 164. len describes the reply and is clamped by the server."},"389":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/cluster-221","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/cluster","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/binary-protocol/cluster#state-transfer","content":"StateChunk (25): nonce 128, offset 144, group 152, artifact 160. Body = raw artifact bytes. Chunks carry no per-chunk integrity; each artifact is verified whole, by length and XxHash3-64, against its manifest entry."},"390":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/cluster-222","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/cluster","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/binary-protocol/cluster#state-transfer","content":"The manifest (little-endian):"},"391":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/cluster-223","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/cluster","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/binary-protocol/cluster#state-transfer","content":"At most 65536 entries. Entries longer than 25 bytes decode with the tail skipped (forward compatibility); shorter entries are rejected."},"392":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/cluster-224","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/cluster","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/binary-protocol/cluster#state-transfer","content":"Artifact kinds:"},"393":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/cluster-225","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/cluster","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/binary-protocol/cluster#state-transfer","content":"Kind"},"394":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/cluster-226","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/cluster","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/binary-protocol/cluster#state-transfer","content":"Artifact"},"395":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/cluster-227","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/cluster","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/binary-protocol/cluster#state-transfer","content":"Plane"},"396":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/cluster-228","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/cluster","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/binary-protocol/cluster#state-transfer","content":"Content"},"397":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/cluster-229","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/cluster","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/binary-protocol/cluster#state-transfer","content":"0"},"398":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/cluster-230","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/cluster","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/binary-protocol/cluster#state-transfer","content":"METADATA_SNAPSHOT"},"399":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/cluster-231","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/cluster","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/binary-protocol/cluster#state-transfer","content":"metadata"},"400":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/cluster-232","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/cluster","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/binary-protocol/cluster#state-transfer","content":"snapshot.bin verbatim (MessagePack, snapshot format version 3); frontier = sequence number"},"401":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/cluster-233","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/cluster","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/binary-protocol/cluster#state-transfer","content":"1"},"402":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/cluster-234","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/cluster","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/binary-protocol/cluster#state-transfer","content":"CLIENT_TABLE"},"403":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/cluster-235","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/cluster","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/binary-protocol/cluster#state-transfer","content":"metadata"},"404":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/cluster-236","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/cluster","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/binary-protocol/cluster#state-transfer","content":"Client table encoding (magic ICT2); frontier = mutation frontier"},"405":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/cluster-237","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/cluster","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/binary-protocol/cluster#state-transfer","content":"2"},"406":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/cluster-238","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/cluster","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/binary-protocol/cluster#state-transfer","content":"SEGMENT_LOG"},"407":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/cluster-239","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/cluster","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/binary-protocol/cluster#state-transfer","content":"partition"},"408":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/cluster-240","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/cluster","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/binary-protocol/cluster#state-transfer","content":"One retained segment's .log verbatim; frontier = segment base offset"},"409":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/cluster-241","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/cluster","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/binary-protocol/cluster#state-transfer","content":"3"},"410":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/cluster-242","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/cluster","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/binary-protocol/cluster#state-transfer","content":"CONSUMER_OFFSETS"},"411":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/cluster-243","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/cluster","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/binary-protocol/cluster#state-transfer","content":"partition"},"412":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/cluster-244","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/cluster","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/binary-protocol/cluster#state-transfer","content":"Consumer + group offset tables and applied purge generation; frontier = offer's commit_op"},"413":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/cluster-245","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/cluster","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/binary-protocol/cluster#session-forwarding","content":"A client may dial a backup; authentication happens there, and only the verified identity travels to the primary (credentials never cross the replica plane):"},"414":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/cluster-246","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/cluster","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/binary-protocol/cluster#session-forwarding","content":"ForwardRegister (26): client 128, nonce 144, user_id 160."},"415":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/cluster-247","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/cluster","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/binary-protocol/cluster#session-forwarding","content":"ForwardRegisterResult (27): nonce 128, client 144, epoch 160, watermark 168, outcome byte at 255: 0 Ok, 1 NotPrimary, 2 NotCaughtUp, 3 PipelineFull, 4 InProgress, 5 Canceled, 6 ClientIdOwnedByAnotherUser. epoch and watermark must be zero on any non-Ok outcome."},"416":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/cluster-248","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/cluster","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/binary-protocol/cluster#session-forwarding","content":"ForwardLogout (28): client 128, nonce 144, session 160, request 168."},"417":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/cluster-249","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/cluster","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/binary-protocol/cluster#session-forwarding","content":"ForwardLogoutResult (29): nonce 128, client 144, commit 160, outcome at 255: 0 Ok, 1 NotPrimary, 2 PipelineFull, 3 InProgress, 4 Canceled."},"418":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/cluster-250","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/cluster","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/binary-protocol/cluster#consensus-planes","content":"Two consensus planes share this one message set; there are no plane-specific commands. Routing is by the group: u64 field carried on every consensus header except the handshake and session-forwarding frames (those are implicitly metadata-plane):"},"419":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/cluster-251","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/cluster","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/binary-protocol/cluster#consensus-planes","content":"group = 1 << 63 is the metadata plane (streams, topics, users, consumer groups; durable on-disk journal)."},"420":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/cluster-252","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/cluster","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/binary-protocol/cluster#consensus-planes","content":"Any other value is a packed stream/topic/partition key: a partition plane group (message batches, consumer offsets; in-memory journal)."},"421":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/cluster-253","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/cluster","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/binary-protocol/cluster#consensus-planes","content":"Same wire, a few divergent semantics: partition-plane prepares leave checksum_body zero and rely on the batch's own batch_checksum, their identity checksum covers the header alone, and StateTransferTarget.unavailable_transient / commit_max are read only by the partition arm."},"422":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands","type":"page","content":"Commands","breadcrumbs":["Documentation","Binary Protocol"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands"},"423":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands-0","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands","content":"The catalog of command codes, their operation bytes, and which commands are replicated."},"424":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands-1","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands#catalog","content":"Catalog"},"425":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands-2","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands#payloads","content":"Payloads"},"426":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands-3","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands#system","content":"System"},"427":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands-4","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands#streams","content":"Streams"},"428":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands-5","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands#topics","content":"Topics"},"429":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands-6","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands#partitions","content":"Partitions"},"430":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands-7","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands#segments","content":"Segments"},"431":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands-8","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands#messages","content":"Messages"},"432":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands-9","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands#consumer-offsets","content":"Consumer offsets"},"433":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands-10","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands#consumer-groups","content":"Consumer groups"},"434":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands-11","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands#users","content":"Users"},"435":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands-12","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands#authentication","content":"Authentication"},"436":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands-13","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands#personal-access-tokens","content":"Personal access tokens"},"437":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands-14","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands#catalog","content":"The u32 command codes. Replicated commands are identified on the wire by their operation byte (see Operation discriminants). The code column below is the protocol-level registry and, for non-replicated commands, the value carried in header bytes 196..200."},"438":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands-15","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands#catalog","content":"FLUSH_UNSAVED_BUFFER still decodes on the wire, but the server has no on-demand flush primitive and answers every call with FeatureUnavailable. Per-topic durability is configured with the enforce_fsync topic option instead."},"439":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands-16","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands#payloads","content":"Payloads below are the request body (the bytes after the 256-byte header). Types reference the shared encodings. Empty payload means the body has zero bytes."},"440":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands-17","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands#system","content":"Ping. Code: 1. Empty payload. The only command accepted before login."},"441":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands-18","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands#system","content":"Get stats. Code: 10. Empty payload."},"442":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands-19","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands#system","content":"Get snapshot. Code: 11."},"443":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands-20","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands#system","content":"Compression codes: 1 = Stored, 2 = Deflated, 3 = Bzip2, 4 = Zstd, 5 = Lzma, 6 = Xz. Snapshot type codes: 1 = FilesystemOverview, 2 = ProcessList, 3 = ResourceUsage, 4 = Test, 5 = ServerLogs, 6 = ServerConfig, 100 = All."},"444":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands-21","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands#system","content":"Get cluster metadata. Code: 12. Empty payload."},"445":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands-22","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands#system","content":"Describe options. Code: 13."},"446":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands-23","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands#system","content":"Scope: 1 = topic, 2 = stream, 3 = user. Returns the option catalog for the scope: keys, value kinds, defaults, and descriptions. See Options block."},"447":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands-24","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands#system","content":"Get me. Code: 20. Empty payload."},"448":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands-25","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands#system","content":"Get client. Code: 21."},"449":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands-26","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands#system","content":"Get clients. Code: 22. Empty payload."},"450":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands-27","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands#streams","content":"Get stream. Code: 200."},"451":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands-28","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands#streams","content":"Get streams. Code: 201. Empty payload."},"452":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands-29","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands#streams","content":"Create stream. Code: 202."},"453":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands-30","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands#streams","content":"The stream option catalog is empty today, so the block is normally empty (zero bytes)."},"454":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands-31","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands#streams","content":"Delete stream. Code: 203."},"455":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands-32","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands#streams","content":"Update stream. Code: 204."},"456":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands-33","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands#streams","content":"Patch semantics: absent option keys are left unchanged."},"457":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands-34","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands#streams","content":"Purge stream. Code: 205."},"458":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands-35","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands#topics","content":"Get topic. Code: 300."},"459":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands-36","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands#topics","content":"Get topics. Code: 301."},"460":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands-37","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands#topics","content":"Create topic. Code: 302."},"461":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands-38","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands#topics","content":"The fixed fields are the shape of the operation: which stream, how many partitions, what name. Every topic setting (compression_algorithm, message_expiry, max_topic_size, segment_size, enforce_fsync, messages_required_to_save, size_of_messages_required_to_save, preallocate_segments) rides the options block. See Topic options. partitions_count is an argument, not a setting: it is consumed at admission and never persisted as an option."},"462":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands-39","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands#topics","content":"Delete topic. Code: 303."},"463":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands-40","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands#topics","content":"Update topic. Code: 304."},"464":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands-41","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands#topics","content":"Only compression_algorithm, message_expiry, and max_topic_size are updatable. Absent keys are left unchanged."},"465":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands-42","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands#topics","content":"Purge topic. Code: 305."},"466":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands-43","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands#partitions","content":"Create partitions. Code: 402."},"467":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands-44","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands#partitions","content":"Delete partitions. Code: 403."},"468":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands-45","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands#segments","content":"Delete segments. Code: 503."},"469":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands-46","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands#segments","content":"Deletes the segments_count oldest sealed segments of the partition."},"470":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands-47","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands#messages","content":"Poll messages. Code: 100."},"471":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands-48","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands#messages","content":"strategy is a polling strategy. count is the requested number of messages. auto_commit = 1 stores the consumer offset server-side as part of the poll."},"472":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands-49","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands#messages","content":"The response body:"},"473":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands-50","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands#messages","content":"The 16-byte prefix is followed by a stream of batch records served as stored: each record's header carries the stamped base_offset and base_timestamp, and each frame's deltas resolve against them. A record may be a server-sliced view of a larger stored batch, so the first polled offset is base_offset + offset_delta of the first frame, not necessarily base_offset itself. current_offset is the partition's newest offset at poll time."},"474":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands-51","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands#messages","content":"Send messages. Code: 101."},"475":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands-52","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands#messages","content":"metadata_length counts the bytes from stream_id through messages_count inclusive, so a reader can skip straight to the batch. The producer leaves partition_id, base_offset, and base_timestamp zero in the batch header, and the server stamps them. Every checksum is producer-computed and verified at admission. The reserved regions must be zero."},"476":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands-53","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands#messages","content":"Flush unsaved buffer. Code: 102."},"477":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands-54","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands#messages","content":"Parses, but the server always answers FeatureUnavailable: there is no on-demand flush primitive. Use the per-topic enforce_fsync option for durability guarantees."},"478":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands-55","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands#consumer-offsets","content":"Get consumer offset. Code: 120."},"479":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands-56","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands#consumer-offsets","content":"Response body (20 bytes):"},"480":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands-57","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands#consumer-offsets","content":"Store consumer offset. Code: 121."},"481":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands-58","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands#consumer-offsets","content":"Delete consumer offset. Code: 122."},"482":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands-59","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands#consumer-offsets","content":"The trailing ack byte is mandatory on both write commands. A payload without it fails to decode. Ack levels:"},"483":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands-60","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands#consumer-offsets","content":"Value"},"484":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands-61","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands#consumer-offsets","content":"Level"},"485":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands-62","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands#consumer-offsets","content":"Meaning"},"486":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands-63","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands#consumer-offsets","content":"0"},"487":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands-64","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands#consumer-offsets","content":"NoAck"},"488":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands-65","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands#consumer-offsets","content":"Leader-local write; respond as soon as local state is updated (the fast path PollMessages auto-commit uses)"},"489":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands-66","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands#consumer-offsets","content":"1"},"490":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands-67","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands#consumer-offsets","content":"Quorum"},"491":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands-68","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands#consumer-offsets","content":"Replicate through partition consensus; respond after a quorum commit (default for explicit writes)"},"492":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands-69","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands#consumer-groups","content":"Get consumer group. Code: 600."},"493":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands-70","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands#consumer-groups","content":"Get consumer groups. Code: 601."},"494":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands-71","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands#consumer-groups","content":"Create consumer group. Code: 602."},"495":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands-72","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands#consumer-groups","content":"Delete consumer group. Code: 603."},"496":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands-73","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands#consumer-groups","content":"Join consumer group. Code: 604."},"497":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands-74","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands#consumer-groups","content":"Leave consumer group. Code: 605."},"498":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands-75","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands#consumer-groups","content":"Sync consumer group. Code: 606."},"499":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands-76","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands#consumer-groups","content":"Read-only: a member asks for its current partition assignment and the group generation, so it can select partitions to poll client-side."},"500":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands-77","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands#users","content":"Get user. Code: 31."},"501":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands-78","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands#users","content":"Get users. Code: 32. Empty payload."},"502":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands-79","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands#users","content":"Create user. Code: 33."},"503":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands-80","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands#users","content":"permissions_len and the permissions bytes are present only when has_permissions = 1. When it is 0 the options block follows immediately. The user option catalog is empty today, so the block is normally empty. status: 1 = active, 2 = inactive."},"504":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands-81","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands#users","content":"Delete user. Code: 34."},"505":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands-82","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands#users","content":"Update user. Code: 35."},"506":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands-83","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands#users","content":"Update permissions. Code: 36."},"507":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands-84","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands#users","content":"As in CreateUser, the length field is conditional: with has_permissions = 0 the payload ends at the flag."},"508":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands-85","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands#users","content":"Change password. Code: 37."},"509":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands-86","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands#authentication","content":"Login and logout are part of the connection lifecycle:"},"510":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands-87","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands#authentication","content":"Login register. Code: 40. Operation::Register. The body is ClientVersionInfo + credentials, described in Login-register."},"511":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands-88","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands#authentication","content":"Login register with PAT. Code: 45. Operation::Register. The body is ClientVersionInfo + token."},"512":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands-89","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands#authentication","content":"Logout user. Code: 39. Operation::Logout. Empty payload."},"513":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands-90","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands#authentication","content":"Codes 38 and 44 are the pre-register login shapes. The server rejects both with a MalformedLogin eviction."},"514":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands-91","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands#personal-access-tokens","content":"Get personal access tokens. Code: 41. Empty payload."},"515":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands-92","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands#personal-access-tokens","content":"Create personal access token. Code: 42."},"516":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands-93","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands#personal-access-tokens","content":"expiry is a duration in microseconds. Two sentinel values exist: 0 (server default, which currently means no expiry) and u64::MAX (never expires)."},"517":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands-94","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands#personal-access-tokens","content":"Delete personal access token. Code: 43."},"518":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/connection-lifecycle","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/connection-lifecycle","type":"page","content":"Connection lifecycle","breadcrumbs":["Documentation","Binary Protocol"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/binary-protocol/connection-lifecycle"},"519":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/connection-lifecycle-0","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/connection-lifecycle","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/binary-protocol/connection-lifecycle","content":"How the protocol version is packed and checked, and the login-register handshake that authenticates a connection."},"520":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/connection-lifecycle-1","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/connection-lifecycle","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/binary-protocol/connection-lifecycle#protocol-version","content":"Protocol version"},"521":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/connection-lifecycle-2","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/connection-lifecycle","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/binary-protocol/connection-lifecycle#login-register","content":"Login-register"},"522":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/connection-lifecycle-3","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/connection-lifecycle","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/binary-protocol/connection-lifecycle#protocol-version","content":"The protocol version is a semver packed into one u32, 10 bits per component (each must be below 1024):"},"523":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/connection-lifecycle-4","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/connection-lifecycle","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/binary-protocol/connection-lifecycle#protocol-version","content":"Integer order equals semver order. The value tracks the iggy_binary_protocol crate release. Under 0.x the compatibility gate is minor-scoped: the server accepts a client whose packed version is at least the server's minimum and whose major.minor is at most the server's. Patch releases never change the wire, so the upper bound ignores patch. Past 1.0.0 the gate follows strict semver (major bump = incompatible)."},"524":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/connection-lifecycle-5","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/connection-lifecycle","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/binary-protocol/connection-lifecycle#login-register","content":"The only way to authenticate is the register handshake: command code 40 (LOGIN_REGISTER, username and password) or 45 (LOGIN_REGISTER_WITH_PAT, personal access token). Both ride operation = 1 (Register) with session = 0 and a freshly minted non-zero client id."},"525":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/connection-lifecycle-6","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/connection-lifecycle","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/binary-protocol/connection-lifecycle#login-register","content":"Both request bodies begin with the ClientVersionInfo prefix, so the server can gate on the version before touching credentials:"},"526":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/connection-lifecycle-7","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/connection-lifecycle","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/binary-protocol/connection-lifecycle#login-register","content":"protocol_version is the packed version of the protocol the client was built against. sdk_name identifies the SDK (for example rust-sdk, go-sdk). sdk_version is the SDK's own build version."},"527":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/connection-lifecycle-8","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/connection-lifecycle","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/binary-protocol/connection-lifecycle#login-register","content":"After the prefix:"},"528":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/connection-lifecycle-9","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/connection-lifecycle","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/binary-protocol/connection-lifecycle#login-register","content":"LOGIN_REGISTER (code 40):"},"529":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/connection-lifecycle-10","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/connection-lifecycle","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/binary-protocol/connection-lifecycle#login-register","content":"LOGIN_REGISTER_WITH_PAT (code 45):"},"530":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/connection-lifecycle-11","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/connection-lifecycle","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/binary-protocol/connection-lifecycle#login-register","content":"An incompatible protocol version is answered with a 256-byte Eviction frame, reason 14 (IncompatibleProtocol), carrying the accepted window at bytes 144 (max) and 148 (min) as packed u32 versions. A body without a decodable ClientVersionInfo prefix gets reason 15 (MalformedLogin) with a zero window. Bad credentials get reasons 9-11. See EvictionHeader for the frame layout."},"531":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/connection-lifecycle-12","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/connection-lifecycle","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/binary-protocol/connection-lifecycle#login-register","content":"A successful login is a normal Reply whose body is:"},"532":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/connection-lifecycle-13","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/connection-lifecycle","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/binary-protocol/connection-lifecycle#login-register","content":"The client stores session and echoes it in every subsequent request header. Logout is command code 39 (LOGOUT_USER), operation = 3 (Logout), empty body."},"533":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/connection-lifecycle-14","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/connection-lifecycle","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/binary-protocol/connection-lifecycle#login-register","content":"The legacy login commands 38 (LOGIN_USER) and 44 (LOGIN_WITH_PERSONAL_ACCESS_TOKEN) are refused: the server answers them with an Eviction frame, reason 15 (MalformedLogin). Only PING is accepted before login. Every other command on an unauthenticated connection is denied."},"534":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/encodings","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/encod