m its start to the end of the payload (no length prefix, and the validator requires exact consumption) and may be empty."},"619":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/encodings-84","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/encodings","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/binary-protocol/encodings#options-block","content":"On top of the TLV walk, options enforce: string keys only (kind 2, valid UTF-8), no duplicate keys, at most 1024 entries, and at most 100000 bytes total."},"620":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/encodings-85","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/encodings","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/binary-protocol/encodings#options-block","content":"Semantics:"},"621":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/encodings-86","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/encodings","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/binary-protocol/encodings#options-block","content":"Create requests resolve absent keys to server defaults and persist the effective values, so GetTopic always shows what is in force."},"622":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/encodings-87","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/encodings","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/binary-protocol/encodings#options-block","content":"Update requests are patches: keys absent from the block are left alone, never reset. A client built before a key existed cannot erase it."},"623":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/encodings-88","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/encodings","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/binary-protocol/encodings#options-block","content":"Unknown keys are rejected at the wire edge, never silently skipped."},"624":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/encodings-89","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/encodings","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/binary-protocol/encodings#options-block","content":"The catalog is discoverable at runtime with DESCRIBE_OPTIONS (code 13), payload [scope: u8] with scope 1 = topic, 2 = stream, 3 = user (HTTP: GET /options/topic). Today only topics have keys. The stream and user catalogs are empty, and any key sent for them is rejected. The topic catalog (segment_size, enforce_fsync, message_expiry, max_topic_size, and the rest) with defaults and constraints is documented on the Topic options page. UpdateTopic accepts only compression_algorithm, message_expiry, and max_topic_size. The storage-layout knobs are create-only."},"625":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/encodings-90","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/encodings","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/binary-protocol/encodings#compression","content":"The compression_algorithm value used by topic options:"},"626":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/encodings-91","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/encodings","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/binary-protocol/encodings#compression","content":"Code / name"},"627":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/encodings-92","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/encodings","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/binary-protocol/encodings#compression","content":"Algorithm"},"628":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/encodings-93","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/encodings","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/binary-protocol/encodings#compression","content":"1, none"},"629":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/encodings-94","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/encodings","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/binary-protocol/encodings#compression","content":"No compression (default)"},"630":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/encodings-95","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/encodings","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/binary-protocol/encodings#compression","content":"2, gzip"},"631":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/encodings-96","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/encodings","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/binary-protocol/encodings#compression","content":"Gzip"},"632":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/encodings-97","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/encodings","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/binary-protocol/encodings#compression","content":"Any other value is rejected. The option is a placeholder today: the value is validated, persisted, and echoed back, but neither the server nor the SDKs compress or decompress payloads yet. To compress today, do it client-side and tag messages via user headers - see the message headers examples in the Iggy repo."},"633":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/framing","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/framing","type":"page","content":"Framing","breadcrumbs":["Documentation","Binary Protocol"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/binary-protocol/framing"},"634":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/framing-0","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/framing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/binary-protocol/framing","content":"The fixed 256-byte header every binary-transport message rides in, and the request, reply and eviction shapes it carries."},"635":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/framing-1","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/framing","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/binary-protocol/framing#requestheader","content":"RequestHeader"},"636":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/framing-2","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/framing","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/binary-protocol/framing#operation-discriminants","content":"Operation discriminants"},"637":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/framing-3","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/framing","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/binary-protocol/framing#request-numbering","content":"Request numbering"},"638":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/framing-4","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/framing","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/binary-protocol/framing#replyheader","content":"ReplyHeader"},"639":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/framing-5","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/framing","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/binary-protocol/framing#result-section","content":"Result section"},"640":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/framing-6","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/framing","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/binary-protocol/framing#evictionheader","content":"EvictionHeader"},"641":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/framing-7","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/framing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/binary-protocol/framing","content":"Every message on a binary transport is a fixed 256-byte header followed by an optional body. The header is a #[repr(C)] struct decoded by pointer cast (zero-copy), so field offsets are fixed and enforced at compile time in the server. Three header shapes cross the client boundary:"},"642":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/framing-8","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/framing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/binary-protocol/framing","content":"RequestHeader: client to server."},"643":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/framing-9","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/framing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/binary-protocol/framing","content":"ReplyHeader: server to client, answers one request."},"644":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/framing-10","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/framing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/binary-protocol/framing","content":"EvictionHeader: server to client, session-terminal rejection with no body."},"645":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/framing-11","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/framing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/binary-protocol/framing","content":"The header shape is identified by the command byte at offset 60. Values a client sends or receives:"},"646":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/framing-12","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/framing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/binary-protocol/framing","content":"command"},"647":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/framing-13","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/framing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/binary-protocol/framing","content":"Value"},"648":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/framing-14","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/framing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/binary-protocol/framing","content":"Direction"},"649":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/framing-15","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/framing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/binary-protocol/framing","content":"Meaning"},"650":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/framing-16","page_id":"/docs/binary-pr