g-216","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/framing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/binary-protocol/framing#operation-discriminants","content":"The planes matter for delivery semantics:"},"851":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/framing-217","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/framing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/binary-protocol/framing#operation-discriminants","content":"Metadata operations replicate through the metadata consensus group. The server deduplicates them by (client, request) and caches replies, so a retried request gets the cached answer instead of a double apply (exactly-once)."},"852":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/framing-218","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/framing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/binary-protocol/framing#operation-discriminants","content":"Partition operations replicate through their partition's consensus group. They are at-least-once: no reply cache, a replay may apply again."},"853":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/framing-219","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/framing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/binary-protocol/framing#operation-discriminants","content":"Non-replicated operations are reads. They bypass consensus and deduplication entirely."},"854":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/framing-220","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/framing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/binary-protocol/framing#request-numbering","content":"request is a per-client counter the server's client table tracks for metadata-plane operations:"},"855":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/framing-221","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/framing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/binary-protocol/framing#request-numbering","content":"Metadata operations must send a strictly increasing request (the SDK advances the counter per metadata request)."},"856":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/framing-222","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/framing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/binary-protocol/framing#request-numbering","content":"Partition operations and non-replicated operations send the current counter value without advancing it. The server doesn't track theirs."},"857":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/framing-223","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/framing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/binary-protocol/framing#request-numbering","content":"The counter is a watermark, not a contiguous sequence: any value above the last accepted one is admissible."},"858":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/framing-224","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/framing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/binary-protocol/framing#request-numbering","content":"session is the fence epoch: the value handed back by the login reply. Every request after login must echo it. When the same client id registers again, the new registration mints a higher epoch and requests carrying the old one are fenced (rejected as zombies)."},"859":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/framing-225","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/framing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/binary-protocol/framing#replyheader","content":"Server to client. 256 bytes, followed by size - 256 bytes of body."},"860":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/framing-226","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/framing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/binary-protocol/framing#replyheader","content":"Offset"},"861":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/framing-227","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/framing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/binary-protocol/framing#replyheader","content":"Size"},"862":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/framing-228","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/framing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/binary-protocol/framing#replyheader","content":"Field"},"863":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/framing-229","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/framing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/binary-protocol/framing#replyheader","content":"Type"},"864":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/framing-230","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/framing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/binary-protocol/framing#replyheader","content":"Description"