,"url":"/docs/connectors/runtime#http-api","content":"GET /sources/{key}/configs/active: active configuration details."},"1822":{"id":"/docs/connectors/runtime-83","page_id":"/docs/connectors/runtime","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/runtime#http-api","content":"PUT /sources/{key}/configs/active: activate a specific configuration version for the source."},"1823":{"id":"/docs/connectors/runtime-84","page_id":"/docs/connectors/runtime","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/runtime#http-api","content":"GET /sources/{key}/configs/plugin: source plugin config, including the optional format query parameter to specify the config format."},"1824":{"id":"/docs/connectors/runtime-85","page_id":"/docs/connectors/runtime","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/runtime#http-api","content":"GET /sources/{key}/transforms: source transforms to be applied to the fields."},"1825":{"id":"/docs/connectors/runtime-86","page_id":"/docs/connectors/runtime","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/runtime#http-api","content":"POST /sources/{key}/restart: restart the source connector."},"1826":{"id":"/docs/connectors/sdk","page_id":"/docs/connectors/sdk","type":"page","content":"SDK","breadcrumbs":["Documentation","Connectors"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/connectors/sdk"},"1827":{"id":"/docs/connectors/sdk-0","page_id":"/docs/connectors/sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/sdk","content":"The Sink and Source traits, the connector macros, and the decoders and encoders used to read and write Iggy streams."},"1828":{"id":"/docs/connectors/sdk-1","page_id":"/docs/connectors/sdk","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/connectors/sdk#traits","content":"Traits"},"1829":{"id":"/docs/connectors/sdk-2","page_id":"/docs/connectors/sdk","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/connectors/sdk#state","content":"State"},"1830":{"id":"/docs/connectors/sdk-3","page_id":"/docs/connectors/sdk","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/connectors/sdk#retry-utilities","content":"Retry utilities"},"1831":{"id":"/docs/connectors/sdk-4","page_id":"/docs/connectors/sdk","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/connectors/sdk#transforms","content":"Transforms"},"1832":{"id":"/docs/connectors/sdk-5","page_id":"/docs/connectors/sdk","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/connectors/sdk#protocol-buffers-support","content":"Protocol Buffers Support"},"1833":{"id":"/docs/connectors/sdk-6","page_id":"/docs/connectors/sdk","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/connectors/sdk#configuration-example","content":"Configuration Example"},"1834":{"id":"/docs/connectors/sdk-7","page_id":"/docs/connectors/sdk","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/connectors/sdk#key-configuration-options","content":"Key Configuration Options"},"1835":{"id":"/docs/connectors/sdk-8","page_id":"/docs/connectors/sdk","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/connectors/sdk#source-configuration","content":"Source Configuration"},"1836":{"id":"/docs/connectors/sdk-9","page_id":"/docs/connectors/sdk","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/connectors/sdk#transform-options","content":"Transform Options"},"1837":{"id":"/docs/connectors/sdk-10","page_id":"/docs/connectors/sdk","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/connectors/sdk#supported-features","content":"Supported Features"},"1838":{"id":"/docs/connectors/sdk-11","page_id":"/docs/connectors/sdk","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/connectors/sdk#programmatic-usage","content":"Programmatic Usage"},"1839":{"id":"/docs/connectors/sdk-12","page_id":"/docs/connectors/sdk","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/connectors/sdk#dynamic-schema-loading","content":"Dynamic Schema Loading"},"1840":{"id":"/docs/connectors/sdk-13","page_id":"/docs/connectors/sdk","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/connectors/sdk#creating-converters-with-schema","content":"Creating Converters with Schema"},"1841":{"id":"/docs/connectors/sdk-14","page_id":"/docs/connectors/sdk","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/connectors/sdk#usage-notes","content":"Usage Notes"},"1842":{"id":"/docs/connectors/sdk-15","page_id":"/docs/connectors/sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/sdk","content":"SDK provides the commonly used structs and traits such as Sink and Source, along with the sink_connector and source_connector macros to be used when developing connectors."},"1843":{"id":"/docs/connectors/sdk-16","page_id":"/docs/connectors/sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/sdk","content":"Moreover, it contains both, the decoders and encoders modules, implementing either StreamDecoder or StreamEncoder traits, which are used when consuming or producing data from/to Iggy streams. All the supported schemas - JSON, Raw bytes, Text, Protocol Buffers, FlatBuffers, and Avro - have both a decoder and an encoder."},"1844":{"id":"/docs/connectors/sdk-17","page_id":"/docs/connectors/sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/sdk#traits","content":"A source produces messages to the configured stream and topic. A sink consumes messages from the configured stream(s) and topic(s):"},"1845":{"id":"/docs/connectors/sdk-18","page_id":"/docs/connectors/sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/sdk#traits","content":"Expose the implementation to the runtime with the matching macro, and provide the expected new() constructor:"},"1846":{"id":"/docs/connectors/sdk-19","page_id":"/docs/connectors/sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/sdk#traits","content":"The macros generate the C FFI symbols loaded by the runtime: iggy_sink_open, iggy_sink_consume, iggy_sink_close, and iggy_sink_version for sinks, and iggy_source_open, iggy_source_handle, iggy_source_close, and iggy_source_version for sources. Data crossing the FFI boundary is serialized using postcard, and each plugin receives its own Tokio runtime inside the SDK."},"1847":{"id":"/docs/connectors/sdk-20","page_id":"/docs/connectors/sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/sdk#state","content":"Source connectors can persist their position between restarts. Each batch of ProducedMessages may carry an optional ConnectorState - the runtime saves it to a file after the batch is sent to Iggy, and passes the last saved state to new() on the next startup. ConnectorState wraps raw bytes and provides serialize/deserialize helpers backed by MessagePack. Sinks don't manage state. Their offsets are tracked by Iggy consumer groups. See the Observability page for where and how state files are stored."},"1848":{"id":"/docs/connectors/sdk-21","page_id":"/docs/connectors/sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/sdk#retry-utilities","content":"The retry module ships helpers for connectors talking to flaky external systems: a CircuitBreaker, an HttpRetryMiddleware for reqwest-based clients, and a build_retry_client factory that wraps a reqwest client with the retry middleware (the circuit breaker is wired separately)."},"1849":{"id":"/docs/connectors/sdk-22","page_id":"/docs/connectors/sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/sdk#transforms","content":"Last but not least, the different transforms are available, to transform (add, update, delete etc.) the particular fields of the data being processed via external configuration. It's as simple as adding a new transform to the transforms section of the particular connector configuration file:"},"1850":{"id":"/docs/connectors/sdk-23","page_id":"/docs/connectors/sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/sdk#transforms","content":"All the available transforms and their configuration options are documented on the Transforms page."},"1851":{"id":"/docs/connectors/sdk-24","page_id":"/docs/connectors/sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/sdk#protocol-buffers-support","content":"The SDK includes support for Protocol Buffers (protobuf) format with both encoding and decoding capabilities. Protocol Buffers provide efficient serialization and are particularly useful for high-performance data streaming scenarios."},"1852":{"id":"/docs/connectors/sdk-25","page_id":"/docs/connectors/sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/sdk#configuration-example","content":"Here's a complete example configuration for using Protocol Buffers with Iggy connectors. No protobuf-specific plugin ships with the runtime, so the example assumes a custom source and sink you implement yourself. The schema = \"proto\" stream setting and the proto_convert transform work with any connector."},"1853":{"id":"/docs/connectors/sdk-26","page_id":"/docs/connectors/sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/sdk#configuration-example","content":"Main runtime config (config.toml):"},"1854":{"id":"/docs/connectors/sdk-27","page_id":"/docs/connectors/sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/sdk#configuration-example","content":"Source connector config (connectors/protobuf_source.toml):"},"1855":{"id":"/docs/connectors/sdk-28","page_id":"/docs/connectors/sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/sdk#configuration-example","content":"Sink connector config (connectors/protobuf_sink.toml):"},"1856":{"id":"/docs/connectors/sdk-29","page_id":"/docs/connectors/sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/sdk#configuration-example","content":"The format-conversion transforms define no per-key defaults. Every non-optional key shown above must be present, or the configuration fails to deserialize (schema_path, message_type, field_mappings, and descriptor_set are the optional ones)."},"1857":{"id":"/docs/connectors/sdk-30","page_id":"/docs/connectors/sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/sdk#configuration-example","content":"The two [[streams]] shapes differ: a source produces to a single topic and can tune batching via batch_length and linger_time, while a sink consumes from a list of topics and can additionally set batch_length, poll_interval, and consumer_group."},"1858":{"id":"/docs/connectors/sdk-31","page_id":"/docs/connectors/sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/sdk#source-configuration","content":"schema_path: Path to the .proto file containing message definitions"},"1859":{"id":"/docs/connectors/sdk-32","page_id":"/docs/connectors/sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/sdk#source-configuration","content":"message_type: Fully qualified name of the protobuf message type to use"},"1860":{"id":"/docs/connectors/sdk-33","page_id":"/docs/connectors/sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/sdk#source-configuration","content":"use_any_wrapper: Whether to wrap messages in google.protobuf.Any for type safety"},"1861":{"id":"/docs/connectors/sdk-34","page_id":"/docs/connectors/sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/sdk#transform-options","content":"proto_convert: Transform for converting between protobuf and other formats"},"1862":{"id":"/docs/connectors/sdk-35","page_id":"/docs/connectors/sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/sdk#transform-options","content":"source_format / target_format: Formats to convert between - any schema value (json, raw, text, proto, flat_buffer, avro)"},"1863":{"id":"/docs/connectors/sdk-36","page_id":"/docs/connectors/sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/sdk#transform-options","content":"preserve_unknown_fields: Whether to keep fields that are not present in the schema during conversion"},"1864":{"id":"/docs/connectors/sdk-37","page_id":"/docs/connectors/sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/sdk#transform-options","content":"include_paths: Additional directories searched for imported .proto files"},"1865":{"id":"/docs/connectors/sdk-38","page_id":"/docs/connectors/sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/sdk#transform-options","content":"field_mappings: Mapping of field names for transformation (e.g., \"old_field\" = \"new_field\")"},"1866":{"id":"/docs/connectors/sdk-39","page_id":"/docs/connectors/sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/sdk#transform-options","content":"conversion_options: Fine-tuning knobs: validate_messages, pretty_json, include_metadata, type_url_prefix, strict_mode"},"1867":{"id":"/docs/connectors/sdk-40","page_id":"/docs/connectors/sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/sdk#transform-options","content":"The schema_registry_url field is reserved and currently not implemented. The SDK never contacts a schema registry, and schemas are loaded only from schema_path or descriptor_set."},"1868":{"id":"/docs/connectors/sdk-41","page_id":"/docs/connectors/sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/sdk#supported-features","content":"Encoding: Convert JSON, Text, and Raw data to protobuf format"},"1869":{"id":"/docs/connectors/sdk-42","page_id":"/docs/connectors/sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/sdk#supported-features","content":"Decoding: Parse protobuf messages into JSON format with type information"},"1870":{"id":"/docs/connectors/sdk-43","page_id":"/docs/connectors/sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/sdk#supported-features","content":"Transforms: Convert between protobuf and other formats"},"1871":{"id":"/docs/connectors/sdk-44","page_id":"/docs/connectors/sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/sdk#supported-features","content":"Field Mapping: Transform field names during format conversion"},"1872":{"id":"/docs/connectors/sdk-45","page_id":"/docs/connectors/sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/sdk#supported-features","content":"Any Wrapper: Support for google.protobuf.Any message wrapper"},"1873":{"id":"/docs/connectors/sdk-46","page_id":"/docs/connectors/sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/sdk#dynamic-schema-loading","content":"You can load or reload schemas programmatically:"},"1874":{"id":"/docs/connectors/sdk-47","page_id":"/docs/connectors/sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/sdk#dynamic-schema-loading","content":"The encoder follows the same pattern:"},"1875":{"id":"/docs/connectors/sdk-48","page_id":"/docs/connectors/sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/sdk#usage-notes","content":"Automatic Loading: Schemas are loaded automatically when schema_path or descriptor_set is provided in config"},"1876":{"id":"/docs/connectors/sdk-49","page_id":"/docs/connectors/sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/sdk#usage-notes","content":"Manual Loading: Use load_schema() method for dynamic schema loading or reloading"},"1877":{"id":"/docs/connectors/sdk-50","page_id":"/docs/connectors/sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/sdk#usage-notes","content":"Error Handling: Schema loading errors are handled gracefully with fallback to Any wrapper mode"},"1878":{"id":"/docs/connectors/sdk-51","page_id":"/docs/connectors/sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/sdk#usage-notes","content":"Immutable Design: Converters are created with fixed configuration - create new instances for different schemas"},"1879":{"id":"/docs/connectors/sdk-52","page_id":"/docs/connectors/sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/sdk#usage-notes","content":"When use_any_wrapper is enabled, messages are wrapped in google.protobuf.Any for better type safety"},"1880":{"id":"/docs/connectors/sdk-53","page_id":"/docs/connectors/sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/sdk#usage-notes","content":"The proto_convert transform can be used to convert protobuf messages to JSON for easier processing"},"1881":{"id":"/docs/connectors/sdk-54","page_id":"/docs/connectors/sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/sdk#usage-notes","content":"Field mappings allow you to rename fields during format conversion"},"1882":{"id":"/docs/connectors/sdk-55","page_id":"/docs/connectors/sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/sdk#usage-notes","content":"Protocol Buffers provide efficient binary serialization compared to JSON"},"1883":{"id":"/docs/connectors/transforms","page_id":"/docs/connectors/transforms","type":"page","content":"Transforms","breadcrumbs":["Documentation","Connectors"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/connectors/transforms"},"1884":{"id":"/docs/connectors/transforms-0","page_id":"/docs/connectors/transforms","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/transforms","content":"Mutate, filter and convert messages as they pass through the connector runtime, and how each transform is configured."},"1885":{"id":"/docs/connectors/transforms-1","page_id":"/docs/connectors/transforms","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/connectors/transforms#configuration","content":"Configuration"},"1886":{"id":"/docs/connectors/transforms-2","page_id":"/docs/connectors/transforms","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/connectors/transforms#add_fields","content":"add_fields"},"1887":{"id":"/docs/connectors/transforms-3","page_id":"/docs/connectors/transforms","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/connectors/transforms#delete_fields","content":"delete_fields"},"1888":{"id":"/docs/connectors/transforms-4","page_id":"/docs/connectors/transforms","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/connectors/transforms#update_fields","content":"update_fields"},"1889":{"id":"/docs/connectors/transforms-5","page_id":"/docs/connectors/transforms","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/connectors/transforms#filter_fields","content":"filter_fields"},"1890":{"id":"/docs/connectors/transforms-6","page_id":"/docs/connectors/transforms","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/connectors/transforms#unwrap_envelope","content":"unwrap_envelope"},"1891":{"id":"/docs/connectors/transforms-7","page_id":"/docs/connectors/transforms","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/connectors/transforms#proto_convert","content":"proto_convert"},"1892":{"id":"/docs/connectors/transforms-8","page_id":"/docs/connectors/transforms","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/connectors/transforms#flat_buffer_convert","content":"flat_buffer_convert"},"1893":{"id":"/docs/connectors/transforms-9","page_id":"/docs/connectors/transforms","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/connectors/transforms#avro_convert","content":"avro_convert"},"1894":{"id":"/docs/connectors/transforms-10","page_id":"/docs/connectors/transforms","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/connectors/transforms#adding-a-new-transform","content":"Adding a new transform"},"1895":{"id":"/docs/connectors/transforms-11","page_id":"/docs/connectors/transforms","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/transforms","content":"Transforms mutate, filter, or convert messages as they flow through the connector runtime. They run inside the runtime process, not inside the plugins. For source connectors they're applied after the plugin's messages are decoded and before producing to Iggy. For sink connectors, after consuming from Iggy and before handing the messages to the plugin."},"1896":{"id":"/docs/connectors/transforms-12","page_id":"/docs/connectors/transforms","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/transforms","content":"A transform receives each decoded message and returns either the (possibly modified) message, or nothing - in which case the message is dropped from the batch."},"1897":{"id":"/docs/connectors/transforms-13","page_id":"/docs/connectors/transforms","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/transforms#configuration","content":"Transforms are configured per connector in the transforms section of the connector configuration file. Each transform type is a table keyed by its name, so a connector can have at most one instance of each type:"},"1898":{"id":"/docs/connectors/transforms-14","page_id":"/docs/connectors/transforms","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/transforms#configuration","content":"Two rules apply to every transform:"},"1899":{"id":"/docs/connectors/transforms-15","page_id":"/docs/connectors/transforms","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/transforms#configuration","content":"The enabled key is required. A transform section without it fails to load, and a transform with enabled = false is skipped."},"1900":{"id":"/docs/connectors/transforms-16","page_id":"/docs/connectors/transforms","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/transforms#configuration","content":"The order of the sections in the file doesn't determine the execution order, so don't rely on one transform seeing the output of another."},"1901":{"id":"/docs/connectors/transforms-17","page_id":"/docs/connectors/transforms","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/transforms#configuration","content":"The field-level transforms (add_fields, delete_fields, filter_fields, update_fields, unwrap_envelope) operate on JSON payloads only. Messages with any other payload format pass through them unchanged."},"1902":{"id":"/docs/connectors/transforms-18","page_id":"/docs/connectors/transforms","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/transforms#add_fields","content":"Adds new fields to the message payload. Each field has a key and a value, which is either static or computed at runtime:"},"1903":{"id":"/docs/connectors/transforms-19","page_id":"/docs/connectors/transforms","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/transforms#add_fields","content":"The available computed values are:"},"1904":{"id":"/docs/connectors/transforms-20","page_id":"/docs/connectors/transforms","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/transforms#add_fields","content":"Value"},"1905":{"id":"/docs/connectors/transforms-21","page_id":"/docs/connectors/transforms","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/transforms#add_fields","content":"Result"},"1906":{"id":"/docs/connectors/transforms-22","page_id":"/docs/connectors/transforms","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/transforms#add_fields","content":"date_time"},"1907":{"id":"/docs/connectors/transforms-23","page_id":"/docs/connectors/transforms","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/transforms#add_fields","content":"Current date and time as an RFC 3339 string"},"1908":{"id":"/docs/connectors/transforms-24","page_id":"/docs/connectors/transforms","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/transforms#add_fields","content":"timestamp_nanos"},"1909":{"id":"/docs/connectors/transforms-25","page_id":"/docs/connectors/transforms","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/transforms#add_fields","content":"Current Unix timestamp in nanoseconds"},"1910":{"id":"/docs/connectors/transforms-26","page_id":"/docs/connectors/transforms","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/transforms#add_fields","content":"timestamp_micros"},"1911":{"id":"/docs/connectors/transforms-27","page_id":"/docs/connectors/transforms","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/transforms#add_fields","content":"Current Unix timestamp in microseconds"},"1912":{"id":"/docs/connectors/transforms-28","page_id":"/docs/connectors/transforms","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/transforms#add_fields","content":"timestamp_millis"},"1913":{"id":"/docs/connectors/transforms-29","page_id":"/docs/connectors/transforms","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/transforms#add_fields","content":"Current Unix timestamp in milliseconds"},"1914":{"id":"/docs/connectors/transforms-30","page_id":"/docs/connectors/transforms","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/transforms#add_fields","content":"timestamp_seconds"},"1915":{"id":"/docs/connectors/transforms-31","page_id":"/docs/connectors/transforms","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/transforms#add_fields","content":"Current Unix timestamp in seconds"},"1916":{"id":"/docs/connectors/transforms-32","page_id":"/docs/connectors/transforms","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/transforms#add_fields","content":"uuid_v4"},"1917":{"id":"/docs/connectors/transforms-33","page_id":"/docs/connectors/transforms","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/transforms#add_fields","content":"Random UUID (version 4)"},"1918":{"id":"/docs/connectors/transforms-34","page_id":"/docs/connectors/transforms","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/transforms#add_fields","content":"uuid_v7"},"1919":{"id":"/docs/connectors/transforms-35","page_id":"/docs/connectors/transforms","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/transforms#add_fields","content":"Time-ordered UUID (version 7)"},"1920":{"id":"/docs/connectors/transforms-36","page_id":"/docs/connectors/transforms","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/transforms#delete_fields","content":"Removes the listed fields from the message payload:"},"1921":{"id":"/docs/connectors/transforms-37","page_id":"/docs/connectors/transforms","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/transforms#update_fields","content":"Sets fields to static or computed values, optionally guarded by a condition:"},"1922":{"id":"/docs/connectors/transforms-38","page_id":"/docs/connectors/transforms","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/transforms#update_fields","content":"The condition values are always (the default when omitted - sets the field whether or not it exists), key_exists (update only when the field is already present), and key_not_exists (add only when the field is absent)."},"1923":{"id":"/docs/connectors/transforms-39","page_id":"/docs/connectors/transforms","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/transforms#filter_fields","content":"Keeps or drops fields of the payload based on key and value patterns:"},"1924":{"id":"/docs/connectors/transforms-40","page_id":"/docs/connectors/transforms","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/transforms#filter_fields","content":"keep_fields: fields that are always kept, regardless of the patterns."},"1925":{"id":"/docs/connectors/transforms-41","page_id":"/docs/connectors/transforms","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/transforms#filter_fields","content":"patterns: a list of patterns. A field matches when any pattern matches. Within one pattern, key_pattern and value_pattern must both match when both are set (an omitted one matches everything)."},"1926":{"id":"/docs/connectors/transforms-42","page_id":"/docs/connectors/transforms","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/transforms#filter_fields","content":"include_matching: true (default) keeps the matching fields and drops the rest. false drops the matching fields and keeps the rest."},"1927":{"id":"/docs/connectors/transforms-43","page_id":"/docs/connectors/transforms","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/transforms#filter_fields","content":"key_pattern variants: exact, starts_with, ends_with, contains, regex - each takes a string, e.g. key_pattern = { contains = \"name\" }."},"1928":{"id":"/docs/connectors/transforms-44","page_id":"/docs/connectors/transforms","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/transforms#filter_fields","content":"value_pattern variants: equals (any JSON value), contains (substring), regex, greater_than, less_than, between (e.g. { between = [1.0, 10.0] }), and the parameterless checks is_null, is_not_null, is_string, is_number, is_boolean, is_object, is_array (written as plain strings, e.g. value_pattern = \"is_number\")."},"1929":{"id":"/docs/connectors/transforms-45","page_id":"/docs/connectors/transforms","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/transforms#unwrap_envelope","content":"Promotes a nested field of a JSON envelope to the top-level payload. Given { \"table\": \"...\", \"operation\": \"...\", \"data\": { ... } }, setting field = \"data\" replaces the whole payload with the contents of data:"},"1930":{"id":"/docs/connectors/transforms-46","page_id":"/docs/connectors/transforms","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/transforms#unwrap_envelope","content":"field must not be empty. Messages that aren't JSON objects, or that don't contain the field, pass through unchanged."},"1931":{"id":"/docs/connectors/transforms-47","page_id":"/docs/connectors/transforms","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/transforms#proto_convert","content":"Converts messages between Protocol Buffers and other formats. See the SDK documentation for the schema loading details:"},"1932":{"id":"/docs/connectors/transforms-48","page_id":"/docs/connectors/transforms","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/transforms#proto_convert","content":"Optional keys: schema_path, message_type, field_mappings (rename fields during conversion, e.g. field_mappings = { \"old\" = \"new\" }), and descriptor_set (pre-compiled descriptor bytes instead of a .proto file)."},"1933":{"id":"/docs/connectors/transforms-49","page_id":"/docs/connectors/transforms","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/transforms#flat_buffer_convert","content":"Converts messages between FlatBuffers and other formats:"},"1934":{"id":"/docs/connectors/transforms-50","page_id":"/docs/connectors/transforms","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/transforms#flat_buffer_convert","content":"Optional keys: schema_path, root_table_name, field_mappings."},"1935":{"id":"/docs/connectors/transforms-51","page_id":"/docs/connectors/transforms","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/transforms#avro_convert","content":"Converts messages between Avro and other formats. The schema can be given inline (schema_json) or as a file (schema_path):"},"1936":{"id":"/docs/connectors/transforms-52","page_id":"/docs/connectors/transforms","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/transforms#avro_convert","content":"Optional keys: schema_path, schema_json, field_mappings."},"1937":{"id":"/docs/connectors/transforms-53","page_id":"/docs/connectors/transforms","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/transforms#avro_convert","content":"Unlike the field-level transforms, the three format-conversion transforms define no per-key defaults: every key shown in their examples (except the ones listed as optional) must be present, or the connector configuration fails to load. The source_format and target_format keys accept any schema value: json, raw, text, proto, flat_buffer, avro. The FlatBuffers transform additionally restricts the pair: only JSON to/from FlatBuffers, FlatBuffers to Text or Raw, and identity conversions are accepted. Any other combination fails the configuration load."},"1938":{"id":"/docs/connectors/transforms-54","page_id":"/docs/connectors/transforms","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/transforms#adding-a-new-transform","content":"The extension point is the SDK (core/connectors/sdk/src/transforms/):"},"1939":{"id":"/docs/connectors/transforms-55","page_id":"/docs/connectors/transforms","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/transforms#adding-a-new-transform","content":"Add a new module implementing the Transform trait."},"1940":{"id":"/docs/connectors/transforms-56","page_id":"/docs/connectors/transforms","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/transforms#adding-a-new-transform","content":"Add a variant to the TransformType enum."},"1941":{"id":"/docs/connectors/transforms-57","page_id":"/docs/connectors/transforms","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/transforms#adding-a-new-transform","content":"Add a match arm to the from_config function deserializing your config."},"1942":{"id":"/docs/connectors/transforms-58","page_id":"/docs/connectors/transforms","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/transforms#adding-a-new-transform","content":"The runtime loads transforms generically by TransformType, so no runtime changes are needed."},"1943":{"id":"/docs/faq/faq","page_id":"/docs/faq/faq","type":"page","content":"FAQ","breadcrumbs":["Documentation","FAQ"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/faq/faq"},"1944":{"id":"/docs/faq/faq-0","page_id":"/docs/faq/faq","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/faq/faq","content":"Common questions about Apache Iggy, including how it compares to Kafka and other message brokers."},"1945":{"id":"/docs/faq/faq-1","page_id":"/docs/faq/faq","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/faq/faq#q-what-is-the-difference-between-iggy-and-traditional-message-brokers-like-kafka","content":"Q: What is the difference between Iggy and traditional message brokers like Kafka?"},"1946":{"id":"/docs/faq/faq-2","page_id":"/docs/faq/faq","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/faq/faq#q-are-there-plans-to-support-kafka-protocol-in-iggy","content":"Q: Are there plans to support Kafka protocol in Iggy?"},"1947":{"id":"/docs/faq/faq-3","page_id":"/docs/faq/faq","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/faq/faq#q-what-transport-protocol-should-i-use","content":"Q: What transport protocol should I use?"},"1948":{"id":"/docs/faq/faq-4","page_id":"/docs/faq/faq","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/faq/faq#q-what-are-the-system-requirements","content":"Q: What are the system requirements?"},"1949":{"id":"/docs/faq/faq-5","page_id":"/docs/faq/faq","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/faq/faq#q-how-does-consumer-group-rebalancing-work","content":"Q: How does consumer group rebalancing work?"},"1950":{"id":"/docs/faq/faq-6","page_id":"/docs/faq/faq","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/faq/faq#q-does-iggy-support-exactly-once-delivery","content":"Q: Does Iggy support exactly-once delivery?"},"1951":{"id":"/docs/faq/faq-7","page_id":"/docs/faq/faq","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/faq/faq#q-how-do-i-secure-my-iggy-deployment","content":"Q: How do I secure my Iggy deployment?"},"1952":{"id":"/docs/faq/faq-8","page_id":"/docs/faq/faq","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/faq/faq#q-can-i-use-iggy-with-my-existing-tooling","content":"Q: Can I use Iggy with my existing tooling?"},"1953":{"id":"/docs/faq/faq-9","page_id":"/docs/faq/faq","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/faq/faq#q-what-happened-to-the-tokio-based-runtime","content":"Q: What happened to the Tokio-based runtime?"},"1954":{"id":"/docs/faq/faq-10","page_id":"/docs/faq/faq","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/faq/faq#q-is-clusteringreplication-available","content":"Q: Is clustering/replication available?"},"1955":{"id":"/docs/faq/faq-11","page_id":"/docs/faq/faq","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/faq/faq#q-what-examples-are-available","content":"Q: What examples are available?"},"1956":{"id":"/docs/faq/faq-12","page_id":"/docs/faq/faq","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/faq/faq#q-what-is-the-difference-between-iggy-and-traditional-message-brokers-like-kafka","content":"Iggy is a persistent message streaming platform that stores messages in an append-only log format, similar to Kafka. However, Iggy is designed for high performance and low latency using io_uring and a thread-per-core architecture. Iggy doesn't use kafka protocol, instead it has its own binary protocol optimized for speed and efficiency, which means that clients need to use our native client libraries."},"1957":{"id":"/docs/faq/faq-13","page_id":"/docs/faq/faq","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/faq/faq#q-are-there-plans-to-support-kafka-protocol-in-iggy","content":"Currently, Iggy does not support the Kafka protocol. Our focus is on providing a high-performance native protocol that leverages the strengths of Iggy's architecture. However, we are open to community contributions (in fact there is an discussion about it)."},"1958":{"id":"/docs/faq/faq-14","page_id":"/docs/faq/faq","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/faq/faq#q-what-transport-protocol-should-i-use","content":"For maximum throughput and lowest latency, use TCP. If you need built-in encryption without configuring TLS separately, QUIC is a good choice. WebSocket works well for browser-based clients. HTTP is the most accessible but has the highest overhead due to JSON serialization and stateless connections (consumer groups can be created, inspected, and deleted, but there is no join/leave membership over HTTP)."},"1959":{"id":"/docs/faq/faq-15","page_id":"/docs/faq/faq","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/faq/faq#q-what-are-the-system-requirements","content":"Iggy is a single binary with no external dependencies. On Linux, it uses io_uring for maximum performance, which requires kernel 5.19+. The server starts with around 20 MB of RAM (plus the configured memory pool, default 4 GiB). For Docker, you need to set SYS_NICE capability, disable seccomp, and set unlimited memlock."},"1960":{"id":"/docs/faq/faq-16","page_id":"/docs/faq/faq","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/faq/faq#q-how-does-consumer-group-rebalancing-work","content":"When consumers join or leave a consumer group, the server triggers cooperative partition rebalancing. Partitions are redistributed among active members. During rebalancing, there is a pending revocation phase (configurable timeout, default 30s) to ensure in-progress message processing can complete before partitions move to new owners."},"1961":{"id":"/docs/faq/faq-17","page_id":"/docs/faq/faq","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/faq/faq#q-does-iggy-support-exactly-once-delivery","content":"Iggy supports at-most-once (with auto-commit) and at-least-once (without auto-commit, manual offset management). Exactly-once semantics can be achieved at the application level, e.g. by attaching unique IDs to messages and deduplicating on the consumer side."},"1962":{"id":"/docs/faq/faq-18","page_id":"/docs/faq/faq","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/faq/faq#q-how-do-i-secure-my-iggy-deployment","content":"Iggy supports TLS on all transport protocols, Argon2id password hashing, granular per-stream/per-topic permissions, Personal Access Tokens for programmatic access, and optional AES-256-GCM encryption at rest. For the HTTP API, JWT tokens are used for session management. See the Security documentation for details."},"1963":{"id":"/docs/faq/faq-19","page_id":"/docs/faq/faq","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/faq/faq#q-can-i-use-iggy-with-my-existing-tooling","content":"Iggy provides a Model Context Protocol (MCP) server with 40+ tools for LLM integration, connectors for piping data to/from external systems (e.g. PostgreSQL, MongoDB, Elasticsearch, ClickHouse, InfluxDB, S3, Delta Lake, Apache Iceberg, Quickwit), Prometheus metrics, and OpenTelemetry traces/logs. The HTTP API works with any REST client."},"1964":{"id":"/docs/faq/faq-20","page_id":"/docs/faq/faq","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/faq/faq#q-what-happened-to-the-tokio-based-runtime","content":"Iggy migrated from Tokio to a thread-per-core architecture with compio (which uses io_uring on Linux) starting with version 0.6.0. The migration delivered major performance improvements, including up to 92% better P9999 tail latency, and an 18% throughput improvement when fsync is enabled. You can read the full story in the thread-per-core io_uring blog post."},"1965":{"id":"/docs/faq/faq-21","page_id":"/docs/faq/faq","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/faq/faq#q-is-clusteringreplication-available","content":"Yes. Replication based on Viewstamped Replication (VSR) is built into the server. Multi-node clustering is configured via the [cluster] section (single-node mode is the default, enabled = false). It covers consensus, view changes, node authentication, and TLS between nodes, and is maturing toward production readiness. See the Clustering documentation for the protocol and Deploy for setting up a cluster."},"1966":{"id":"/docs/faq/faq-22","page_id":"/docs/faq/faq","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/faq/faq#q-what-examples-are-available","content":"Examples are available in the repository for multiple languages: Rust (basic, getting-started, message-envelope, message-headers, multi-tenant, new-sdk, sink-data-producer, stream-builder, tcp-tls), C#, Go, Java, Node.js, PHP, and Python. Docker Compose files and Helm charts are also provided."},"1967":{"id":"/docs/introduction/about","page_id":"/docs/introduction/about","type":"page","content":"About","breadcrumbs":["Documentation","Introduction"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/introduction/about"},"1968":{"id":"/docs/introduction/about-0","page_id":"/docs/introduction/about","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/introduction/about","content":"What Apache Iggy is: a persistent message streaming platform written in Rust, with QUIC, TCP, WebSocket and HTTP transports."},"1969":{"id":"/docs/introduction/about-1","page_id":"/docs/introduction/about","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/introduction/about#features","content":"Features"},"1970":{"id":"/docs/introduction/about-2","page_id":"/docs/introduction/about","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/introduction/about#supported-languages-sdk","content":"Supported languages SDK"},"1971":{"id":"/docs/introduction/about-3","page_id":"/docs/introduction/about","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/introduction/about#cli","content":"CLI"},"1972":{"id":"/docs/introduction/about-4","page_id":"/docs/introduction/about","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/introduction/about#web-ui","content":"Web UI"},"1973":{"id":"/docs/introduction/about-5","page_id":"/docs/introduction/about","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/introduction/about#connectors","content":"Connectors"},"1974":{"id":"/docs/introduction/about-6","page_id":"/docs/introduction/about","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/introduction/about#model-context-protocol","content":"Model Context Protocol"},"1975":{"id":"/docs/introduction/about-7","page_id":"/docs/introduction/about","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/introduction/about#docker","content":"Docker"},"1976":{"id":"/docs/introduction/about-8","page_id":"/docs/introduction/about","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/introduction/about#helm-charts","content":"Helm Charts"},"1977":{"id":"/docs/introduction/about-9","page_id":"/docs/introduction/about","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/introduction/about#versioning","content":"Versioning"},"1978":{"id":"/docs/introduction/about-10","page_id":"/docs/introduction/about","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/introduction/about","content":"Iggy is a persistent message streaming platform written in Rust, supporting QUIC, TCP, WebSocket (custom binary specification) and HTTP (regular REST API) transport protocols, capable of processing millions of messages per second at ultra-low latency."},"1979":{"id":"/docs/introduction/about-11","page_id":"/docs/introduction/about","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/introduction/about","content":"Iggy provides exceptionally high throughput and performance while utilizing minimal computing resources."},"1980":{"id":"/docs/introduction/about-12","page_id":"/docs/introduction/about","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/introduction/about","content":"This is not yet another extension running on top of existing infrastructure, such as Kafka or SQL database."},"1981":{"id":"/docs/introduction/about-13","page_id":"/docs/introduction/about","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/introduction/about","content":"Iggy is a persistent message streaming log built from the ground up using low-level I/O with thread-per-core shared nothing architecture, io_uring and compio for maximum speed and efficiency."},"1982":{"id":"/docs/introduction/about-14","page_id":"/docs/introduction/about","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/introduction/about","content":"The name is an abbreviation for the Italian Greyhound - small yet extremely fast dogs, the best in their class. See the lovely Fabio & Cookie ❤️"},"1983":{"id":"/docs/introduction/about-15","page_id":"/docs/introduction/about","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/introduction/about#features","content":"Highly performant, persistent append-only log for message streaming"},"1984":{"id":"/docs/introduction/about-16","page_id":"/docs/introduction/about","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/introduction/about#features","content":"Very high throughput for both writes and reads"},"1985":{"id":"/docs/introduction/about-17","page_id":"/docs/introduction/about","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/introduction/about#features","content":"Low latency and predictable resource usage thanks to the Rust compiled language (no GC) and io_uring"},"1986":{"id":"/docs/introduction/about-18","page_id":"/docs/introduction/about","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/introduction/about#features","content":"Thread per core shared nothing design together with io_uring guarantee the best possible performance on modern Linux systems"},"1987":{"id":"/docs/introduction/about-19","page_id":"/docs/introduction/about","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/introduction/about#features","content":"NUMA-aware sharding with CPU affinity and memory binding per NUMA node for optimal hardware utilization"},"1988":{"id":"/docs/introduction/about-20","page_id":"/docs/introduction/about","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/introduction/about#features","content":"User authentication and authorization with granular permissions, Personal Access Tokens (PAT) and Argon2id password hashing"},"1989":{"id":"/docs/introduction/about-21","page_id":"/docs/introduction/about","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/introduction/about#features","content":"Support for multiple streams, topics and partitions"},"1990":{"id":"/docs/introduction/about-22","page_id":"/docs/introduction/about","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/introduction/about#features","content":"Support for multiple transport protocols (QUIC, TCP, WebSocket, HTTP)"},"1991":{"id":"/docs/introduction/about-23","page_id":"/docs/introduction/about","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/introduction/about#features","content":"Fully operational RESTful API which can be optionally enabled"},"1992":{"id":"/docs/introduction/about-24","page_id":"/docs/introduction/about","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/introduction/about#features","content":"Available client SDK in multiple languages"},"1993":{"id":"/docs/introduction/about-25","page_id":"/docs/introduction/about","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/introduction/about#features","content":"Works directly with binary data, avoiding enforced schema and serialization/deserialization overhead"},"1994":{"id":"/docs/introduction/about-26","page_id":"/docs/introduction/about","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/introduction/about#features","content":"Custom zero-copy (de)serialization, which greatly improves the performance and reduces memory usage"},"1995":{"id":"/docs/introduction/about-27","page_id":"/docs/introduction/about","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/introduction/about#features","content":"Custom memory pool with 28 buckets (buffer sizes from 4 KiB to 512 MiB) for pre-allocated, zero-copy message passing"},"1996":{"id":"/docs/introduction/about-28","page_id":"/docs/introduction/about","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/introduction/about#features","content":"Configurable server features (e.g. caching, segment size, data flush interval, transport protocols etc.)"},"1997":{"id":"/docs/introduction/about-29","page_id":"/docs/introduction/about","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/introduction/about#features","content":"Server-side storage of consumer offsets"},"1998":{"id":"/docs/introduction/about-30","page_id":"/docs/introduction/about","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/introduction/about#features","content":"Multiple ways of polling the messages:"},"1999":{"id":"/docs/introduction/about-31","page_id":"/docs/introduction/about","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/introduction/about#features","content":"By offset (using the indexes)"},"2000":{"id":"/docs/introduction/about-32","page_id":"/docs/introduction/about","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/introduction/about#features","content":"By timestamp (using the time indexes)"},"2001":{"id":"/docs/introduction/about-33","page_id":"/docs/introduction/about","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/introduction/about#features","content":"First/Last N messages"},"2002":{"id":"/docs/introduction/about-34","page_id":"/docs/introduction/about","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/introduction/about#features","content":"Next N messages for the specific consumer"},"2003":{"id":"/docs/introduction/about-35","page_id":"/docs/introduction/about","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/introduction/about#features","content":"Possibility of auto committing the offset (e.g. to achieve at-most-once delivery)"},"2004":{"id":"/docs/introduction/about-36","page_id":"/docs/introduction/about","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/introduction/about#features","content":"Consumer groups providing the message ordering and horizontal scaling across the connected clients, with cooperative partition rebalancing"},"2005":{"id":"/docs/introduction/about-37","page_id":"/docs/introduction/about","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/introduction/about#features","content":"Message expiry with auto deletion based on the configurable retention policy"},"2006":{"id":"/docs/introduction/about-38","page_id":"/docs/introduction/about","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/introduction/about#features","content":"Multi-tenant support via abstraction of streams which group topics"},"2007":{"id":"/docs/introduction/about-39","page_id":"/docs/introduction/about","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/introduction/about#features","content":"TLS support for all transport protocols (TCP, QUIC, WebSocket, HTTPS)"},"2008":{"id":"/docs/introduction/about-40","page_id":"/docs/introduction/about","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/introduction/about#features","content":"Connectors - sinks, sources and data transformations based on the custom Rust plugins loaded dynamically at runtime"},"2009":{"id":"/docs/introduction/about-41","page_id":"/docs/introduction/about","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/introduction/about#features","content":"Model Context Protocol - provide context to LLM with MCP server exposing 40+ tools for full Iggy management"},"2010":{"id":"/docs/introduction/about-42","page_id":"/docs/introduction/about","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/introduction/about#features","content":"Optional server-side as well as client-side data encryption using AES-256-GCM"},"2011":{"id":"/docs/introduction/about-43","page_id":"/docs/introduction/about","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/introduction/about#features","content":"Optional metadata support in the form of message headers"},"2012":{"id":"/docs/introduction/about-44","page_id":"/docs/introduction/about","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/introduction/about#features","content":"Support for OpenTelemetry logs & traces + Prometheus metrics"},"2013":{"id":"/docs/introduction/about-45","page_id":"/docs/introduction/about","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/introduction/about#features","content":"Built-in CLI to manage the streaming server installable via cargo install iggy-cli"},"2014":{"id":"/docs/introduction/about-46","page_id":"/docs/introduction/about","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/introduction/about#features","content":"Built-in Web UI dashboard (Svelte) that can be embedded directly in the server binary or run as a standalone container"},"2015":{"id":"/docs/introduction/about-47","page_id":"/docs/introduction/about","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/introduction/about#features","content":"Built-in benchmarking app to test the performance"},"2016":{"id":"/docs/introduction/about-48","page_id":"/docs/introduction/about","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/introduction/about#features","content":"Single binary deployment (no external dependencies)"},"2017":{"id":"/docs/introduction/about-49","page_id":"/docs/introduction/about","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/introduction/about#features","content":"Accept-time connection distribution across shards via file descriptor transfer for load balancing"},"2018":{"id":"/docs/introduction/about-50","page_id":"/docs/introduction/about","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/introduction/about#features","content":"Built on Viewstamped Replication (VSR) consensus: runs as a single node by default, with multi-node clustering available via the [cluster] configuration"},"2019":{"id":"/docs/introduction/about-51","page_id":"/docs/introduction/about","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/introduction/about#supported-languages-sdk","content":"Language"},"2020":{"id":"/docs/introduction/about-52","page_id":"/