nt":"Starting cursor timestamp on first run"},"3653":{"id":"/docs/connectors/sources/influxdb-53","page_id":"/docs/connectors/sources/influxdb","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/sources/influxdb#common-options","content":"payload_format"},"3654":{"id":"/docs/connectors/sources/influxdb-54","page_id":"/docs/connectors/sources/influxdb","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/sources/influxdb#common-options","content":"string"},"3655":{"id":"/docs/connectors/sources/influxdb-55","page_id":"/docs/connectors/sources/influxdb","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/sources/influxdb#common-options","content":"json"},"3656":{"id":"/docs/connectors/sources/influxdb-56","page_id":"/docs/connectors/sources/influxdb","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/sources/influxdb#common-options","content":"json, text, or raw"},"3657":{"id":"/docs/connectors/sources/influxdb-57","page_id":"/docs/connectors/sources/influxdb","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/sources/influxdb#common-options","content":"include_metadata"},"3658":{"id":"/docs/connectors/sources/influxdb-58","page_id":"/docs/connectors/sources/influxdb","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/sources/influxdb#common-options","content":"bool"},"3659":{"id":"/docs/connectors/sources/influxdb-59","page_id":"/docs/connectors/sources/influxdb","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/sources/influxdb#common-options","content":"true"},"3660":{"id":"/docs/connectors/sources/influxdb-60","page_id":"/docs/connectors/sources/influxdb","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/sources/influxdb#common-options","content":"Include all row columns in the payload"},"3661":{"id":"/docs/connectors/sources/influxdb-61","page_id":"/docs/connectors/sources/influxdb","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/sources/influxdb#common-options","content":"stuck_batch_cap_factor"},"3662":{"id":"/docs/connectors/sources/influxdb-62","page_id":"/docs/connectors/sources/influxdb","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/sources/influxdb#common-options","content":"u32"},"3663":{"id":"/docs/connectors/sources/influxdb-63","page_id":"/docs/connectors/sources/influxdb","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/sources/influxdb#common-options","content":"10"},"3664":{"id":"/docs/connectors/sources/influxdb-64","page_id":"/docs/connectors/sources/influxdb","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/sources/influxdb#common-options","content":"V3 only: cap on batch inflation for tied timestamps (max 100, 0 disables)"},"3665":{"id":"/docs/connectors/sources/influxdb-65","page_id":"/docs/connectors/sources/influxdb","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/sources/influxdb#common-options","content":"Further options cover payload_column (extract a single column as the payload, required for text and raw formats) and resilience: timeout (10s), max_retries (3), retry_delay / retry_max_delay, startup health-check retries, and a circuit breaker (circuit_breaker_threshold 5, circuit_breaker_cool_down 30s). See the upstream influxdb_source README for the full list."},"3666":{"id":"/docs/connectors/sources/influxdb-66","page_id":"/docs/connectors/sources/influxdb","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/sources/influxdb#cursor-based-polling","content":"On each poll the cursor (an RFC 3339 timestamp) is substituted into the query template. After a successful batch it advances to the highest timestamp seen. The two versions differ deliberately:"},"3667":{"id":"/docs/connectors/sources/influxdb-67","page_id":"/docs/connectors/sources/influxdb","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/sources/influxdb#cursor-based-polling","content":"V2 uses inclusive >= semantics, so the query must sort by the cursor field (|> sort(columns: [\"_time\"])). Startup fails for >=-style queries lacking a sort. range()-style queries pass that check without a sort, but should keep it anyway, since the skip count below depends on ordered rows. Rows at the cursor timestamp are re-fetched after an advance, and the connector skips exactly the already-delivered ones using a persisted row count, preventing duplicates."},"3668":{"id":"/docs/connectors/sources/influxdb-68","page_id":"/docs/connectors/sources/influxdb","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/sources/influxdb#cursor-based-polling","content":"V3 uses strict WHERE time > '$cursor', so no rows are re-delivered across batches. Because InfluxDB 3's query engine has no stable order for rows sharing one timestamp, the connector pages through tied rows with OFFSET $offset, inflating the batch size up to stuck_batch_cap_factor times batch_size before tripping the circuit breaker."},"3669":{"id":"/docs/connectors/sources/influxdb-69","page_id":"/docs/connectors/sources/influxdb","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/sources/influxdb#payload-formats","content":"json (default): each row becomes a JSON object. V3 emits flat rows with native JSON types. V2 wraps each row in an envelope (measurement, field, timestamp, value, row) with all values as strings coerced to bool/int/float where possible, since annotated CSV carries no types. A string field containing \"42\" arrives as the number 42 under V2 (coerced) but stays the string \"42\" under V3. Values that can't be coerced stay strings under V2. Migrating consumers must update deserialization."},"3670":{"id":"/docs/connectors/sources/influxdb-70","page_id":"/docs/connectors/sources/influxdb","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/sources/influxdb#payload-formats","content":"text: the value of payload_column is produced as a UTF-8 string."},"3671":{"id":"/docs/connectors/sources/influxdb-71","page_id":"/docs/connectors/sources/influxdb","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/sources/influxdb#payload-formats","content":"raw: the value of payload_column is base64-decoded and produced as raw bytes."},"3672":{"id":"/docs/connectors/sources/postgres","page_id":"/docs/connector