ync(...) yourself, await it, then dispose the batch (using var batch = builder.Build())."},"5027":{"id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk-113","page_id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk#publishing-with-rented-batches","content":"After disposal, batch.Messages and every payload's Span throw ObjectDisposedException instead of reading recycled pool memory."},"5028":{"id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk-114","page_id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk#publishing-with-rented-batches","content":"Dispose the builder only when abandoning it before Build(); a using on the builder is safe either way (it becomes a no-op after Build())."},"5029":{"id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk-115","page_id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk#typed-publisher","content":"For automatic object serialization, use IggyPublisherBuilder<T> with an ISerializer<T>:"},"5030":{"id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk-116","page_id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk#typed-publisher","content":"Besides the collection overload, SendAsync also accepts a single item (SendAsync(order, messageId: null, userHeaders: null)) or a collection of (data, messageId, userHeaders) tuples when you need per-message ids or headers."},"5031":{"id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk-117","page_id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk#typed-publisher","content":"For JSON you don't need a custom serializer - the built-in SystemTextJsonSerializer<T> (in Apache.Iggy.Publishers) writes System.Text.Json output directly into the send buffer, with optional JsonSerializerOptions:"},"5032":{"id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk-118","page_id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk#typed-publisher","content":"There is no built-in deserializer counterpart - consumers implement IDeserializer<T> themselves (see Typed consumer)."},"5033":{"id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk-119","page_id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk#iggyconsumer","content":"Configure a consumer, initialize it, then iterate. ReceiveAsync returns an IAsyncEnumerable<ReceivedMessage>:"},"5034":{"id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk-120","page_id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk#consumer-builder-options","content":"Method"},"5035":{"id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk-121","page_id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk#consumer-builder-options","content":"Description"},"5036":{"id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk-122","page_id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk#consumer-builder-options","content":"WithConnection(protocol, address, login, password, receiveBufferSize = 4096, sendBufferSize = 4096, reconnectionSettings = null)"},"5037":{"id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk-123","page_id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk#consumer-builder-options","content":"Connection settings - only used when the builder creates its own client. The builder-created client auto-logs in with these credentials and replays them across reconnects"},"5038":{"id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk-124","page_id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk#consumer-builder-options","content":"WithConnection(protocol, address, personalAccessToken, receiveBufferSize = 4096, sendBufferSize = 4096, reconnectionSettings = null)"},"5039":{"id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk-125","page_id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk#consumer-builder-options","content":"Same, authenticating with a personal access token instead of a username and password"},"5040":{"id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk-126","page_id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk#consumer-builder-options","content":"WithPartitionId(partitionId)"},"5041":{"id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk-127","page_id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk#consumer-builder-options","content":"Consume from a specific partition"},"5042":{"id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk-128","page_id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk#consumer-builder-options","content":"WithPollingStrategy(pollingStrategy)"},"5043":{"id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk-129","page_id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk#consumer-builder-options","content":"Where to start consuming (default: Offset(0)). An Offset(...) strategy is advanced client-side after each poll; other strategies (e.g. Next()) are sent as-is and rely on server-side offset tracking"},"5044":{"id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk-130","page_id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk#consumer-builder-options","content":"WithBatchSize(batchSize)"},"5045":{"id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk-131","page_id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk#consumer-builder-options","content":"Messages fetched per poll (default: 100)"},"5046":{"id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk-132","page_id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk#consumer-builder-options","content":"WithAutoCommitMode(mode)"},"5047":{"id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk-133","page_id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk#consumer-builder-options","content":"Offset auto-commit behavior (see below)"},"5048":{"id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk-134","page_id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk#consumer-builder-options","content":"WithConsumerGroup(groupName, createIfNotExists = true, joinGroup = true)"},"5049":{"id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk-135","page_id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk#consumer-builder-options","content":"Create and/or join a consumer group"},"5050":{"id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk-136","page_id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk#consumer-builder-options","content":"WithPollingInterval(interval)"},"5051":{"id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk-137","page_id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk#consumer-builder-options","content":"Delay between polls to throttle server requests (default: 100ms); TimeSpan.Zero disables throttling"},"5052":{"id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk-138","page_id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk#consumer-builder-options","content":"WithEncryptor(encryptor)"},"5053":{"id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk-139","page_id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk#consumer-builder-options","content":"Client-side payload decryption. Only valid on a builder-created client - for an external client, set IggyClientConfigurator.MessageEncryptor instead. Cannot be combined with AutoCommitMode.Auto (the builder throws - a decryption failure would silently skip an already-committed batch)"},"5054":{"id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk-140","page_id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk#consumer-builder-options","content":"SubscribeOnPollingError(handler)"},"5055":{"id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk-141","page_id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk#consumer-builder-options","content":"Observe polling errors"},"5056":{"id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk-142","page_id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk#consumer-builder-options","content":"WithLogger(loggerFactory)"},"5057":{"id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk-143","page_id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk#consumer-builder-options","content":"Logger factory for diagnostics"},"5058":{"id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk-144","page_id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk#consumer-builder-options","content":"Auto-commit modes (AutoCommitMode):"},"5059":{"id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk-145","page_id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk#consumer-builder-options","content":"Mode"},"5060":{"id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk-146","page_id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk#consumer-builder-options","content":"Description"},"5061":{"id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk-147","page_id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk#consumer-builder-options","content":"Auto"},"5062":{"id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk-148","page_id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk#consumer-builder-options","content":"Commit the offset while polling"},"5063":{"id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk-149","page_id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk#consumer-builder-options","content":"AfterReceive"},"5064":{"id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk-150","page_id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk#consumer-builder-options","content":"Commit after each message is received"},"5065":{"id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk-151","page_id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk#consumer-builder-options","content":"Disabled"},"5066":{"id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk-152","page_id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk#consumer-builder-options","content":"Commit manually"},"5067":{"id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk-153","page_id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk#manual-offset-control","content":"With AutoCommitMode.Disabled, commit offsets yourself via the consumer's StoreOffsetAsync:"},"5068":{"id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk-154","page_id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk#manual-offset-control","content":"StoreOffsetAsync(offset, partitionId, resetLastPolled = false) stores the offset for a partition; pass resetLastPolled: true to also move the consumer's cached last-polled position so the next poll resumes past the stored offset. DeleteOffsetAsync(partitionId) clears the stored offset."},"5069":{"id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk-155","page_id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk#consumer-groups","content":"Pass a Consumer.Group(...) and let the builder create and join the group for load-balanced consumption:"},"5070":{"id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk-156","page_id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk#consuming-with-rented-buffers","content":"ReceiveAsync copies each payload into its own byte[]. ReceiveRentedAsync is the high-level counterpart of the low-level rented poll API: payloads are slices of a pooled buffer shared by all messages from the same poll. Each yielded ReceivedRentedMessage must be disposed - the buffer returns to the pool once the last message of its batch is disposed:"},"5071":{"id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk-157","page_id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk#consuming-with-rented-buffers","content":"ReceivedRentedMessage (IDisposable) exposes Message (a RentedMessageResponse), CurrentOffset, PartitionId, Status, and Error. This path performs no deserialization, so Status is always Success. Payload and raw-header memory are only valid until the message is disposed - copy out anything you need to keep (e.g. message.Message.Payload.ToArray())."},"5072":{"id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk-158","page_id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk#consuming-with-rented-buffers","content":"Auto-commit modes apply exactly as with ReceiveAsync. Forgetting to dispose a message never corrupts data - the batch buffer simply isn't returned to the pool and is reclaimed by the GC as an ordinary allocation."},"5073":{"id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk-159","page_id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk#typed-consumer","content":"For automatic deserialization, use IggyConsumerBuilder<T> with an IDeserializer<T> and iterate with ReceiveDeserializedAsync. Each ReceivedMessage<T> carries a Status you should check:"},"5074":{"id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk-160","page_id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk#typed-consumer","content":"Note: On the typed builders, the fluent With* methods are inherited from the untyped base builder and return the base type, while the typed Build() hides the base one. Chaining Create(...).WithAutoCommitMode(...).Build() therefore resolves to the base Build() and returns an untyped consumer/publisher. Keep the typed builder in a variable (as above) and call Build() on it - the With* calls mutate the builder, so their return value can be ignored."},"5075":{"id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro","page_id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro","type":"page","content":"C# SDK","breadcrumbs":["Documentation","SDK","C#"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro"},"5076":{"id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro-0","page_id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro","content":"The async-first C# SDK for .NET applications over TCP and HTTP, from installation to creating a client."},"5077":{"id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro-1","page_id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro#installation","content":"Installation"},"5078":{"id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro-2","page_id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro#supported-protocols","content":"Supported protocols"},"5079":{"id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro-3","page_id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro#connection-semantics","content":"Connection semantics"},"5080":{"id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro-4","page_id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro#failed-requests","content":"Failed requests"},"5081":{"id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro-5","page_id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro#creating-a-client","content":"Creating a client"},"5082":{"id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro-6","page_id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro#quick-start","content":"Quick start"},"5083":{"id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro-7","page_id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro#producer","content":"Producer"},"5084":{"id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro-8","page_id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro#consumer","content":"Consumer"},"5085":{"id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro-9","page_id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro#next-steps","content":"Next steps"},"5086":{"id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro-10","page_id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro","content":"The Iggy SDK for C# is a modern, async-first client library for interacting with an Iggy message streaming server from your .NET applications. It supports TCP and HTTP transports. The package is available on NuGet and the source code lives on GitHub."},"5087":{"id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro-11","page_id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro","content":"The SDK is built around the IIggyClient interface, which aggregates every feature (publishing, consuming, stream/topic management, users, offsets, consumer groups, and system operations). For the low-level per-call API and the full configuration reference, see the Guide. For the ergonomic, batteries-included producer/consumer abstractions, see the High-level SDK."},"5088":{"id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro-12","page_id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro#installation","content":"The SDK targets .NET 8 and .NET 10 (net8.0 and net10.0). Stable versions on NuGet stop at 0.8.0, which predates the current wire protocol and cannot talk to a current server. The 0.9.0 line ships as prerelease versions (0.9.0-edge.x at the time of writing), so --prerelease is required."},"5089":{"id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro-13","page_id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro#supported-protocols","content":"The SDK supports two transport protocols:"},"5090":{"id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro-14","page_id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro#supported-protocols","content":"TCP - binary protocol for optimal performance and lower latency (recommended). Over TCP the SDK speaks the VSR consensus framing, the only wire protocol the server accepts. See Connection semantics"},"5091":{"id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro-15","page_id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro#supported-protocols","content":"HTTP - RESTful JSON API for stateless operations. BaseAddress must then be a full URI such as http://127.0.0.1:3000 (the server's default HTTP port is 3000, TCP is 8090)"},"5092":{"id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro-16","page_id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro#supported-protocols","content":"Some operations are TCP-only and throw FeatureUnavailableException on HTTP: joining/leaving a consumer group, GetMeAsync, DeleteSegmentsAsync, and SendBinaryRequestAsync."},"5093":{"id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro-17","page_id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro#connection-semantics","content":"Over TCP every request is wrapped in a 256-byte consensus header (Viewstamped Replication), the client registers a consensus session at login, and writes are replicated before they are acknowledged. The IIggyClient surface is unchanged. What changes is the behavior around connections, groups, and failures:"},"5094":{"id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro-18","page_id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro#connection-semantics","content":"Login binds a session. LoginUserAsync / LoginWithPersonalAccessTokenAsync run the register handshake, and the session lives for as long as the connection. Logging out, being evicted, or losing the connection ends it, and the next login registers a fresh one."},"5095":{"id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro-19","page_id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro#connection-semantics","content":"Leader redirection is automatic. The client reads the cluster roster, follows the current leader, and re-checks it when a request is refused because the node stopped being primary. GetCurrentAddress() reports the node the client currently talks to."},"5096":{"id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro-20","page_id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro#connection-semantics","content":"The client picks partitions. Balanced and message-key partitioning are resolved client-side (the message-key hash matches the Rust SDK byte for byte), and consumer-group polls round-robin over the partitions the coordinator assigned to this client."},"5097":{"id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro-21","page_id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro#connection-semantics","content":"Consumer groups are assignment-based. JoinConsumerGroupAsync makes this client a member. The assignment is synced on demand and refreshed on every PingAsync. The TCP client pings on its own every HeartbeatInterval (5 seconds by default), so an idle session survives the server's heartbeat verification and assignments stay fresh."},"5098":{"id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro-22","page_id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro#connection-semantics","content":"Credentials are bounds-checked locally. A username outside 3-50 bytes, a password outside 3-100 bytes, or a personal access token outside 1-255 bytes is rejected before the request is framed."},"5099":{"id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro-23","page_id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro#connection-semantics","content":"Consumer offsets need an explicit partition. StoreOffsetAsync / DeleteOffsetAsync do not accept a null partition id under VSR. Passing one throws client-side."},"5100":{"id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro-24","page_id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro#connection-semantics","content":"Polling a topic that does not exist returns an empty poll rather than throwing. The server answers an unresolved topic with the empty-poll reply shape, so the client cannot tell it apart from a topic with no messages. Check the topic exists first if the distinction matters."},"5101":{"id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro-25","page_id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro#failed-requests","content":"The SDK replays a request whenever the server says it never admitted it. Two exceptions surface to the caller:"},"5102":{"id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro-26","page_id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro#failed-requests","content":"IggyInvalidStatusCodeException carries the server status code, with FromServer telling apart a verdict the cluster reported from a failure the client raised itself."},"5103":{"id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro-27","page_id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro#failed-requests","content":"VsrRequestOutcomeUnknownException means no server verdict arrived after the request was written (the connection was lost, the call was cancelled, or the server evicted the session mid-flight), so the cluster may or may not have committed it. The SDK will not replay it on a new session, because that would bypass server-side deduplication. Re-issuing it is the caller's decision."},"5104":{"id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro-28","page_id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro#failed-requests","content":"The high-level abstractions handle the unknown-outcome case differently:"},"5105":{"id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro-29","page_id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro#failed-requests","content":"IggyConsumer rethrows it rather than swallowing it, because an auto-committing poll may already have advanced the offset. Rethrowing ends the polling loop, so catch it around the enumeration, decide whether the operation is safe to re-issue, and start consuming again:"},"5106":{"id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro-30","page_id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro#failed-requests","content":"IggyPublisher with background sending does not retry it: the batch is reported through the message-batch-failed event (SubscribeOnMessageBatchFailed), typed so a subscriber can tell \"not sent\" from \"possibly sent twice\". Direct (non-background) sends throw it to the caller."},"5107":{"id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro-31","page_id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro#creating-a-client","content":"Create a client with IggyClientFactory.CreateClient, then call ConnectAsync:"},"5108":{"id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro-32","page_id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro#creating-a-client","content":"Optionally, provide an ILoggerFactory for diagnostics (defaults to NullLoggerFactory.Instance):"},"5109":{"id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro-33","page_id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro#creating-a-client","content":"IggyClientConfigurator also exposes buffer sizes, TLS, the heartbeat interval, the maximum response frame size, automatic reconnection with exponential backoff (on by default), auto-login (so you can skip the explicit LoginUserAsync call), and client-side message encryption. See Client configuration for the full reference."},"5110":{"id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro-34","page_id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro#quick-start","content":"These samples use the High-level SDK - the recommended way to build producers and consumers. For the equivalent low-level, per-call flow, see the Guide."},"5111":{"id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro-35","page_id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro#consumer","content":"ReceiveAsync polls indefinitely - pass a CancellationToken or break out of the loop to stop."},"5112":{"id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro-36","page_id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro#next-steps","content":"Guide - client configuration reference and the full API surface: auth, streams, topics, partitions, publishing, consuming, offsets, consumer groups, system operations"},"5113":{"id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro-37","page_id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro#next-steps","content":"High-level SDK - IggyPublisher / IggyConsumer with background sending, retries, auto-commit, typed (de)serialization, and pooled (rented) buffers for allocation-free hot paths"},"5114":{"id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro-38","page_id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro#next-steps","content":"Examples - producer, consumer-group, and typed-message samples, plus links to runnable projects"},"5115":{"id":"/docs/sdk/go/examples","page_id":"/docs/sdk/go/examples","type":"page","content":"Examples","breadcrumbs":["Documentation","SDK","Go"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/sdk/go/examples"},"5116":{"id":"/docs/sdk/go/examples-0","page_id":"/docs/sdk/go/examples","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/go/examples","content":"Runnable Go examples from the core repository, and how to start a server they can connect to."},"5117":{"id":"/docs/sdk/go/examples-1","page_id":"/docs/sdk/go/examples","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/sdk/go/examples#starting-the-server","content":"Starting the server"},"5118":{"id":"/docs/sdk/go/examples-2","page_id":"/docs/sdk/go/examples","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/sdk/go/examples#getting-started","content":"Getting started"},"5119":{"id":"/docs/sdk/go/examples-3","page_id":"/docs/sdk/go/examples","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/sdk/go/examples#tls","content":"TLS"},"5120":{"id":"/docs/sdk/go/examples-4","page_id":"/docs/sdk/go/examples","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/go/examples","content":"A runnable getting-started example lives in the examples/go directory of the core repository. Its go.mod carries a replace directive pointing at the in-repo SDK, so the example always builds against the SDK source in the same checkout. The examples are exercised in CI via scripts/run-examples-from-readme.sh --language go."},"5121":{"id":"/docs/sdk/go/examples-5","page_id":"/docs/sdk/go/examples","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/go/examples#starting-the-server","content":"The Go SDK speaks the VSR (Viewstamped Replication) wire protocol, so the examples run against the VSR server. The examples log in as iggy/iggy, and root credentials are applied only on the very first startup, when no data directory exists yet. Start the server with the default root credentials:"},"5122":{"id":"/docs/sdk/go/examples-6","page_id":"/docs/sdk/go/examples","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/go/examples#starting-the-server","content":"If an example fails to log in, the server data directory (local_data by default) was created with different credentials: delete it and start the server again with the defaults. This setup is intended only for development and testing."},"5123":{"id":"/docs/sdk/go/examples-7","page_id":"/docs/sdk/go/examples","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/go/examples#getting-started","content":"The producer creates sample-stream and sample-topic (if missing) and sends 5 batches of 10 messages to partition 0. The consumer polls them from offset 0 and exits after 5 batches. Stream, topic, and partition IDs are 0-based."},"5124":{"id":"/docs/sdk/go/examples-8","page_id":"/docs/sdk/go/examples","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/go/examples#getting-started","content":"Run both from the examples/go directory:"},"5125":{"id":"/docs/sdk/go/examples-9","page_id":"/docs/sdk/go/examples","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/go/examples#getting-started","content":"Run multiple producers and consumers at once to see how messages are distributed across clients."},"5126":{"id":"/docs/sdk/go/examples-10","page_id":"/docs/sdk/go/examples","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/go/examples#getting-started","content":"Both binaries accept the same flags:"},"5127":{"id":"/docs/sdk/go/examples-11","page_id":"/docs/sdk/go/examples","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/go/examples#getting-started","content":"--tcp-server-address - server address, default 127.0.0.1:8090"},"5128":{"id":"/docs/sdk/go/examples-12","page_id":"/docs/sdk/go/examples","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/go/examples#getting-started","content":"--tls - enable TLS"},"5129":{"id":"/docs/sdk/go/examples-13","page_id":"/docs/sdk/go/examples","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/go/examples#getting-started","content":"--tls-ca-file - path to a CA certificate file"},"5130":{"id":"/docs/sdk/go/examples-14","page_id":"/docs/sdk/go/examples","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/go/examples#getting-started","content":"--tls-domain - TLS server domain name for SNI"},"5131":{"id":"/docs/sdk/go/examples-15","page_id":"/docs/sdk/go/examples","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/go/examples#tls","content":"Start the server with TLS enabled, using the development certificates from the repository:"},"5132":{"id":"/docs/sdk/go/examples-16","page_id":"/docs/sdk/go/examples","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/go/examples#tls","content":"Then run the examples with the TLS flags (paths relative to examples/go):"},"5133":{"id":"/docs/sdk/go/examples-17","page_id":"/docs/sdk/go/examples","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/go/examples#tls","content":"For the code walkthrough, see the quick start on the intro page, which follows the same example."},"5134":{"id":"/docs/sdk/go/intro","page_id":"/docs/sdk/go/intro","type":"page","content":"Go SDK","breadcrumbs":["Documentation","SDK","Go"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/sdk/go/intro"},"5135":{"id":"/docs/sdk/go/intro-0","page_id":"/docs/sdk/go/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/go/intro","content":"The Go SDK, a blocking client speaking the VSR wire protocol over TCP, and which servers it pairs with."},"5136":{"id":"/docs/sdk/go/intro-1","page_id":"/docs/sdk/go/intro","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/sdk/go/intro#server-compatibility","content":"Server compatibility"},"5137":{"id":"/docs/sdk/go/intro-2","page_id":"/docs/sdk/go/intro","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/sdk/go/intro#installation","content":"Installation"},"5138":{"id":"/docs/sdk/go/intro-3","page_id":"/docs/sdk/go/intro","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/sdk/go/intro#quick-start","content":"Quick start"},"5139":{"id":"/docs/sdk/go/intro-4","page_id":"/docs/sdk/go/intro","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/sdk/go/intro#producer","content":"Producer"},"5140":{"id":"/docs/sdk/go/intro-5","page_id":"/docs/sdk/go/intro","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/sdk/go/intro#consumer","content":"Consumer"},"5141":{"id":"/docs/sdk/go/intro-6","page_id":"/docs/sdk/go/intro","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/sdk/go/intro#tls","content":"TLS"},"5142":{"id":"/docs/sdk/go/intro-7","page_id":"/docs/sdk/go/intro","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/sdk/go/intro#delivery-semantics","content":"Delivery semantics"},"5143":{"id":"/docs/sdk/go/intro-8","page_id":"/docs/sdk/go/intro","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/sdk/go/intro#consumer-groups","content":"Consumer groups"},"5144":{"id":"/docs/sdk/go/intro-9","page_id":"/docs/sdk/go/intro","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/sdk/go/intro#examples","content":"Examples"},"5145":{"id":"/docs/sdk/go/intro-10","page_id":"/docs/sdk/go/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/go/intro","content":"The Iggy Go SDK is a client library for interacting with the Iggy server from Go applications. It is a blocking client that speaks the VSR (Viewstamped Replication) wire protocol over TCP, with or without TLS. The package is available on pkg.go.dev and the source code lives in foreign/go."},"5146":{"id":"/docs/sdk/go/intro-11","page_id":"/docs/sdk/go/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/go/intro#server-compatibility","content":"The SDK and the server must speak the same wire protocol. Tagged releases up to v0.8.0 predate VSR (and the current API) and pair only with servers of the same era. The module on master speaks VSR only and requires a current server. When working from source, build the SDK and the server from the same repository checkout. The code on this page tracks master."},"5147":{"id":"/docs/sdk/go/intro-12","page_id":"/docs/sdk/go/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/go/intro#installation","content":"go get resolves to the latest tagged release. Until a post-VSR release is tagged, track master to talk to a current server:"},"5148":{"id":"/docs/sdk/go/intro-13","page_id":"/docs/sdk/go/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/go/intro#quick-start","content":"The snippets below follow the getting-started example. Stream, topic, and partition IDs are 0-based: the first partition of a topic is partition 0."},"5149":{"id":"/docs/sdk/go/intro-14","page_id":"/docs/sdk/go/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/go/intro#producer","content":"CreateTopic accepts optional trailing iggcon.HeaderEntry values carrying named topic options, for option keys the server catalog gained after the SDK build shipped. DescribeOptions lists the keys a server accepts."},"5150":{"id":"/docs/sdk/go/intro-15","page_id":"/docs/sdk/go/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/go/intro#consumer","content":"Polled message payloads and user headers alias the reply buffer, so retaining one message pins the whole reply. Copy the bytes out when they outlive the poll."},"5151":{"id":"/docs/sdk/go/intro-16","page_id":"/docs/sdk/go/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/go/intro#tls","content":"Wrap the TCP options with tcp.WithTLS:"},"5152":{"id":"/docs/sdk/go/intro-17","page_id":"/docs/sdk/go/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/go/intro#tls","content":"tcp.WithTLSValidateCertificate controls certificate validation. The client also accepts client.WithLogger for a custom *slog.Logger and runs a built-in heartbeat loop (every 5 seconds by default) once connected."},"5153":{"id":"/docs/sdk/go/intro-18","page_id":"/docs/sdk/go/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/go/intro#delivery-semantics","content":"SendMessages returns the placements the server committed. Delivery is at-least-once. A send whose reply is lost to a dropped connection returns ErrDisconnected without a replay: a reconnect registers a fresh client identity, so the server could not deduplicate the replay against a batch that may have already committed. Retrying such a send is the caller's decision and may write the batch twice. Consumers that need exactly-once handling deduplicate on the message id. A confirmation reports an in-memory commit, not a flush to disk, and an empty confirmation list is a valid success."},"5154":{"id":"/docs/sdk/go/intro-19","page_id":"/docs/sdk/go/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/go/intro#consumer-groups","content":"The client exposes the full consumer group API: CreateConsumerGroup, JoinConsumerGroup, LeaveConsumerGroup, GetConsumerGroups, and SyncConsumerGroup (which returns the partitions this client currently owns together with the group generation). To poll as a group member, build the consumer with iggcon.NewGroupConsumer and pass a nil partition id to PollMessages. The client orchestrates the group poll. An empty batch with no assigned partition means nothing to read right now (a member that owns no partition, or a rebalance still settling): poll again. ErrConsumerGroupMemberNotFound means the client is not a member, for example after an explicit LeaveConsumerGroup. JoinConsumerGroup restores membership."},"5155":{"id":"/docs/sdk/go/intro-20","page_id":"/docs/sdk/go/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/go/intro#examples","content":"A runnable getting-started example with producer, consumer, and TLS flags lives in examples/go. See the examples page for how to run it."},"5156":{"id":"/docs/sdk/java/examples","page_id":"/docs/sdk/java/examples","type":"page","content":"Examples","breadcrumbs":["Documentation","SDK","Java"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/sdk/java/examples"},"5157":{"id":"/docs/sdk/java/examples-0","page_id":"/docs/sdk/java/examples","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/java/examples","content":"Runnable Java examples from the core repository, built with Gradle, and how to start a server for them."},"5158":{"id":"/docs/sdk/java/examples-1","page_id":"/docs/sdk/java/examples","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/sdk/java/examples#starting-the-server","content":"Starting the server"},"5159":{"id":"/docs/sdk/java/examples-2","page_id":"/docs/sdk/java/examples","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/sdk/java/examples#running-the-examples","content":"Running the examples"},"5160":{"id":"/docs/sdk/java/examples-3","page_id":"/docs/sdk/java/examples","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/sdk/java/examples#blocking-vs-async","content":"Blocking vs. async"},"5161":{"id":"/docs/sdk/java/examples-4","page_id":"/docs/sdk/java/examples","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/java/examples","content":"Runnable examples live in the examples/java directory of the core repository as a standalone Gradle project. The project builds against the in-repo SDK (via an includeBuild substitution), so the examples always match the SDK source in the same checkout. Java 17 is recommended. The included gradlew wrapper downloads the pinned Gradle version on first run."},"5162":{"id":"/docs/sdk/java/examples-5","page_id":"/docs/sdk/java/examples","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/java/examples#starting-the-server","content":"The Java SDK speaks the VSR (Viewstamped Replication) wire protocol, so the examples run against the VSR server. The examples log in as iggy/iggy, and root credentials are applied only on the very first startup, when no data directory exists yet. Start the server with the default root credentials:"},"5163":{"id":"/docs/sdk/java/examples-6","page_id":"/docs/sdk/java/examples","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/java/examples#starting-the-server","content":"Alternatively, set IGGY_ROOT_USERNAME=iggy and IGGY_ROOT_PASSWORD=iggy before the first start. If an example fails with InvalidCredentials, the server data directory (local_data by default) was created with different credentials: delete it and start the server again with the defaults. This setup is intended only for development and testing."},"5164":{"id":"/docs/sdk/java/examples-7","page_id":"/docs/sdk/java/examples","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/java/examples#running-the-examples","content":"Run each example from the examples/java directory with its Gradle task:"},"5165":{"id":"/docs/sdk/java/examples-8","page_id":"/docs/sdk/java/examples","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/java/examples#running-the-examples","content":"gettingstarted - basic blocking producer and consumer, the best starting point."},"5166":{"id":"/docs/sdk/java/examples-9","page_id":"/docs/sdk/java/examples","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/java/examples#running-the-examples","content":"messageheaders - message metadata via custom header keys and values, with header-based routing instead of payload-based typing."},"5167":{"id":"/docs/sdk/java/examples-10","page_id":"/docs/sdk/java/examples","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/java/examples#running-the-examples","content":"messageenvelope - JSON envelope pattern for polymorphic message handling (order events wrapped in envelopes for type identification)."},"5168":{"id":"/docs/sdk/java/examples-11","page_id":"/docs/sdk/java/examples","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/java/examples#running-the-examples","content":"multitenant - multi-tenant isolation: per-tenant streams, users with stream-specific permissions, and concurrent producers and consumers across tenants."},"5169":{"id":"/docs/sdk/java/examples-12","page_id":"/docs/sdk/java/examples","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/java/examples#running-the-examples","content":"sinkdataproducer - high-volume data generation (1000+ messages per batch) with realistic records, for testing and benchmarking."},"5170":{"id":"/docs/sdk/java/examples-13","page_id":"/docs/sdk/java/examples","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/java/examples#running-the-examples","content":"streambuilder - a combined producer and consumer in a single class."},"5171":{"id":"/docs/sdk/java/examples-14","page_id":"/docs/sdk/java/examples","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/java/examples#running-the-examples","content":"async - the async client: CompletableFuture chaining, submitting multiple sends without blocking, backpressure, error recovery with exponential backoff, and thread pool separation."},"5172":{"id":"/docs/sdk/java/examples-15","page_id":"/docs/sdk/java/examples","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/java/examples#running-the-examples","content":"tcptls - TLS-encrypted TCP connections with CA certificate verification. Requires a TLS-enabled server:"},"5173":{"id":"/docs/sdk/java/examples-16","page_id":"/docs/sdk/java/examples","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/java/examples#blocking-vs-async","content":"Use the blocking client for scripts, CLI tools, integration tests, and anywhere sequential code is easier to reason about. Use the async client for high throughput, reactive applications, and composing non-blocking requests with CompletableFuture. The async client runs I/O on Netty event loop threads: never block them with .join(), .get(), Thread.sleep(), or blocking I/O inside thenApply/thenAccept. Offload blocking work with thenApplyAsync(fn, executor). The examples/java README covers these async patterns in detail."},"5174":{"id":"/docs/sdk/java/intro","page_id":"/docs/sdk/java/intro","type":"page","content":"Java SDK","breadcrumbs":["Documentation","SDK","Java"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/sdk/java/intro"},"5175":{"id":"/docs/sdk/java/intro-0","page_id":"/docs/sdk/java/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/java/intro","content":"The Java SDK, with blocking and CompletableFuture clients over TCP, and which servers they pair with."},"5176":{"id":"/docs/sdk/java/intro-1","page_id":"/docs/sdk/java/intro","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/sdk/java/intro#server-compatibility","content":"Server compatibility"},"5177":{"id":"/docs/sdk/java/intro-2","page_id":"/docs/sdk/java/intro","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/sdk/java/intro#installation","content":"Installation"},"5178":{"id":"/docs/sdk/java/intro-3","page_id":"/docs/sdk/java/intro","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/sdk/java/intro#maven","content":"Maven"},"5179":{"id":"/docs/sdk/java/intro-4","page_id":"/docs/sdk/java/intro","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/sdk/java/intro#gradle","content":"Gradle"},"5180":{"id":"/docs/sdk/java/intro-5","page_id":"/docs/sdk/java/intro","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/sdk/java/intro#quick-start","content":"Quick start"},"5181":{"id":"/docs/sdk/java/intro-6","page_id":"/docs/sdk/java/intro","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/sdk/java/intro#producer","content":"Producer"},"5182":{"id":"/docs/sdk/java/intro-7","page_id":"/docs/sdk/java/intro","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/sdk/java/intro#consumer","content":"Consumer"},"5183":{"id":"/docs/sdk/java/intro-8","page_id":"/docs/sdk/java/intro","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/sdk/java/intro#client-types-and-configuration","content":"Client types and configuration"},"5184":{"id":"/docs/sdk/java/intro-9","page_id":"/docs/sdk/java/intro","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/sdk/java/intro#examples","content":"Examples"},"5185":{"id":"/docs/sdk/java/intro-10","page_id":"/docs/sdk/java/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/java/intro","content":"The Iggy Java SDK is a client library for interacting with the Iggy server from Java applications. It provides a blocking client and an async client (based on CompletableFuture) over TCP, plus a blocking HTTP client. The artifact is published to Maven Central and the source code lives in foreign/java."},"5186":{"id":"/docs/sdk/java/intro-11","page_id":"/docs/sdk/java/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/java/intro#server-compatibility","content":"The TCP transport speaks the VSR (Viewstamped Replication) wire protocol. The SDK and the server must speak the same protocol: the latest published artifact (0.6.0) predates VSR and only pairs with servers of the same era, while the SDK on master requires a current server. When working from source, build the SDK and the server from the same repository checkout. The code on this page tracks master."},"5187":{"id":"/docs/sdk/java/intro-12","page_id":"/docs/sdk/java/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/java/intro#gradle","content":"Check Maven Central for the latest published version. Snapshot builds are available from the ASF snapshot repository."},"5188":{"id":"/docs/sdk/java/intro-13","page_id":"/docs/sdk/java/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/java/intro#quick-start","content":"The snippets below follow the getting-started example. Stream, topic, and partition IDs are 0-based: the first partition of a topic is partition 0."},"5189":{"id":"/docs/sdk/java/intro-14","page_id":"/docs/sdk/java/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/java/intro#producer","content":"createTopic also has an overload with a trailing Map<String, HeaderValue> of named topic options, for option keys the server catalog gained after the SDK build shipped."},"5190":{"id":"/docs/sdk/java/intro-15","page_id":"/docs/sdk/java/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/java/intro#client-types-and-configuration","content":"org.apache.iggy.Iggy is the unified entrypoint for building clients. Iggy.tcpClientBuilder().blocking() returns the same builder as IggyTcpClient.builder() used above."},"5191":{"id":"/docs/sdk/java/intro-16","page_id":"/docs/sdk/java/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/java/intro#client-types-and-configuration","content":"The TCP builder also accepts TLS and resilience options:"},"5192":{"id":"/docs/sdk/java/intro-17","page_id":"/docs/sdk/java/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/java/intro#client-types-and-configuration","content":"Beyond streams, topics, and messages, the client exposes consumer groups, partitions, users, and personal access tokens through client.consumerGroups(), client.partitions(), client.users(), and client.personalAccessTokens(). All SDK exceptions inherit from IggyException."},"5193":{"id":"/docs/sdk/java/intro-18","page_id":"/docs/sdk/java/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/java/intro#examples","content":"Runnable examples covering the async client, message headers and envelopes, multi-tenant setups, and TLS live in examples/java. See the examples page for how to run them."},"5194":{"id":"/docs/sdk/node/examples","page_id":"/docs/sdk/node/examples","type":"page","content":"Examples","breadcrumbs":["Documentation","SDK","Node"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/sdk/node/examples"},"5195":{"id":"/docs/sdk/node/examples-0","page_id":"/docs/sdk/node/examples","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/node/examples","content":"TypeScript examples for the Node.js SDK, exercised by CI so they match the current SDK."},"5196":{"id":"/docs/sdk/node/examples-1","page_id":"/docs/sdk/node/examples","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/sdk/node/examples#running-the-examples","content":"Running the examples"},"5197":{"id":"/docs/sdk/node/examples-2","page_id":"/docs/sdk/node/examples","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/node/examples","content":"Working examples are available in the examples/node directory, written in TypeScript. CI exercises them, so they always match the current SDK:"},"5198":{"id":"/docs/sdk/node/examples-3","page_id":"/docs/sdk/node/examples","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/node/examples","content":"getting-started - basic producer and consumer"},"5199":{"id":"/docs/sdk/node/examples-4","page_id":"/docs/sdk/node/examples","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/node/examples","content":"basic - producer and consumer with utilities"},"5200":{"id":"/docs/sdk/node/examples-5","page_id":"/docs/sdk/node/examples","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/node/examples","content":"message-envelope - JSON message envelope pattern"},"5201":{"id":"/docs/sdk/node/examples-6","page_id":"/docs/sdk/node/examples","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/node/examples","content":"message-headers - custom message headers"},"5202":{"id":"/docs/sdk/node/examples-7","page_id":"/docs/sdk/node/examples","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/node/examples","content":"multi-tenant - multi-tenant streaming setup"},"5203":{"id":"/docs/sdk/node/examples-8","page_id":"/docs/sdk/node/examples","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/node/examples","content":"tcp-tls - TLS-encrypted TCP connections"},"5204":{"id":"/docs/sdk/node/examples-9","page_id":"/docs/sdk/node/examples","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/node/examples","content":"stream-builder - end-to-end walkthrough: create a stream and topic, produce, and consume"},"5205":{"id":"/docs/sdk/node/examples-10","page_id":"/docs/sdk/node/examples","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/node/examples","content":"sink-data-producer - bulk data generation for sink connectors"},"5206":{"id":"/docs/sdk/node/examples-11","page_id":"/docs/sdk/node/examples","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/node/examples#running-the-examples","content":"Start an Iggy server first:"},"5207":{"id":"/docs/sdk/node/examples-12","page_id":"/docs/sdk/node/examples","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/node/examples#running-the-examples","content":"The environment variables make the server reachable through the published port (it binds to 127.0.0.1 inside the container by default) and set the iggy/iggy root credentials the examples default to."},"5208":{"id":"/docs/sdk/node/examples-13","page_id":"/docs/sdk/node/examples","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/node/examples#running-the-examples","content":"Then, from examples/node, install the dependencies and run a producer/consumer pair:"},"5209":{"id":"/docs/sdk/node/examples-14","page_id":"/docs/sdk/node/examples","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/node/examples#running-the-examples","content":"Each example set has a matching test:<name> script in examples/node/package.json."},"5210":{"id":"/docs/sdk/node/examples-15","page_id":"/docs/sdk/node/examples","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/node/examples#running-the-examples","content":"For the quick-start code itself, see the Node.js SDK intro."},"5211":{"id":"/docs/sdk/node/intro","page_id":"/docs/sdk/node/intro","type":"page","content":"Node.js SDK","breadcrumbs":["Documentation","SDK","Node"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/sdk/node/intro"},"5212":{"id":"/docs/sdk/node/intro-0","page_id":"/docs/sdk/node/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/node/intro","content":"The Node.js and TypeScript SDK, from installation to keeping client and server versions in step."},"5213":{"id":"/docs/sdk/node/intro-1","page_id":"/docs/sdk/node/intro","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/sdk/node/intro#installation","content":"Installation"},"5214":{"id":"/docs/sdk/node/intro-2","page_id":"/docs/sdk/node/intro","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/sdk/node/intro#quick-start","content":"Quick start"},"5215":{"id":"/docs/sdk/node/intro-3","page_id":"/docs/sdk/node/intro","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/sdk/node/intro#producer","content":"Producer"},"5216":{"id":"/docs/sdk/node/intro-4","page_id":"/docs/sdk/node/intro","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/sdk/node/intro#consumer","content":"Consumer"},"5217":{"id":"/docs/sdk/node/intro-5","page_id":"/docs/sdk/node/intro","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/sdk/node/intro#configuration","content":"Configuration"},"5218":{"id":"/docs/sdk/node/intro-6","page_id":"/docs/sdk/node/intro","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/sdk/node/intro#beyond-the-basics","content":"Beyond the basics"},"5219":{"id":"/docs/sdk/node/intro-7","page_id":"/docs/sdk/node/intro","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/sdk/node/intro#examples","content":"Examples"},"5220":{"id":"/docs/sdk/node/intro-8","page_id":"/docs/sdk/node/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/node/intro","content":"The Iggy Node.js SDK is a client library that allows you to interact with the Iggy API from your Node.js and TypeScript applications. It communicates with the Iggy server over TCP or TLS using the binary protocol. The package is available on npm and the source code can be found on GitHub."},"5221":{"id":"/docs/sdk/node/intro-9","page_id":"/docs/sdk/node/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/node/intro","content":"The SDK speaks only the current (VSR) wire protocol and doesn't fall back to older formats. Keep the client and server versions in step: pair the newest package with the newest server."},"5222":{"id":"/docs/sdk/node/intro-10","page_id":"/docs/sdk/node/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/node/intro#quick-start","content":"The samples below expect an Iggy server on 127.0.0.1:8090:"},"5223":{"id":"/docs/sdk/node/intro-11","page_id":"/docs/sdk/node/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/node/intro#quick-start","content":"The environment variables make the server reachable through the published port (it binds to 127.0.0.1 inside the container by default) and set the root credentials the samples log in with. Without --with-default-root-credentials (or the IGGY_ROOT_USERNAME / IGGY_ROOT_PASSWORD variables), a first boot generates a random root password instead of iggy/iggy."},"5224":{"id":"/docs/sdk/node/intro-12","page_id":"/docs/sdk/node/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/node/intro#producer","content":"The Node SDK sends the partitioning choice on the wire, and the server resolves balanced and key routing at admission (the Rust, C#, Java, and Go clients instead resolve them client-side)."},"5225":{"id":"/docs/sdk/node/intro-13","page_id":"/docs/sdk/node/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/node/intro#configuration","content":"The client constructor accepts a few options:"},"5226":{"id":"/docs/sdk/node/intro-14","page_id":"/docs/sdk/node/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/node/intro#configuration","content":"reconnect: automatic reconnection with { enabled, interval, maxRetries }."},"5227":{"id":"/docs/sdk/node/intro-15","page_id":"/docs/sdk/node/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/node/intro#configuration","content":"heartbeatInterval: the client pings the server every 5 seconds by default. 0 disables heartbeats. The server evicts connections that stay silent too long, so keep it well below the server's heartbeat timeout."},"5228":{"id":"/docs/sdk/node/intro-16","page_id":"/docs/sdk/node/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/node/intro#configuration","content":"maxResponseFrameSize: response frames larger than this limit (default 64 MiB) are rejected and close the connection. Raise it when polling very large batches."},"5229":{"id":"/docs/sdk/node/intro-17","page_id":"/docs/sdk/node/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/node/intro#configuration","content":"poolSize: the client currently supports exactly one pooled connection. min/max above 1 throw."},"5230":{"id":"/docs/sdk/node/intro-18","page_id":"/docs/sdk/node/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/node/intro#configuration","content":"TLS: set transport: 'TLS' and pass Node tls.ConnectionOptions in options. See the tcp-tls example."},"5231":{"id":"/docs/sdk/node/intro-19","page_id":"/docs/sdk/node/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/node/intro#beyond-the-basics","content":"Consumer groups: client.group manages groups (including ensure-and-join in one call), and polling with Consumer.Group distributes partitions across group members."},"5232":{"id":"/docs/sdk/node/intro-20","page_id":"/docs/sdk/node/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/node/intro#beyond-the-basics","content":"Consumer streams: singleConsumerStream and groupConsumerStream expose polling as Node streams."},"5233":{"id":"/docs/sdk/node/intro-21","page_id":"/docs/sdk/node/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/node/intro#beyond-the-basics","content":"Administration: users, personal access tokens, offsets, partitions, segments, and cluster metadata are all available on the client."},"5234":{"id":"/docs/sdk/node/intro-22","page_id":"/docs/sdk/node/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/node/intro#beyond-the-basics","content":"Errors: the package exports ResponseError, DeserializeError, ProtocolFrameError, and VsrEvictionError for precise error handling."},"5235":{"id":"/docs/sdk/node/intro-23","page_id":"/docs/sdk/node/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/node/intro#beyond-the-basics","content":"See the foreign/node README for the full API walkthrough."},"5236":{"id":"/docs/sdk/node/intro-24","page_id":"/docs/sdk/node/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/node/intro#examples","content":"Working examples are available in the examples/node directory, written in TypeScript. See Examples for the list and how to run them."},"5237":{"id":"/docs/sdk/php/intro","page_id":"/docs/sdk/php/intro","type":"page","content":"PHP SDK","breadcrumbs":["Documentation","SDK","PHP"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/sdk/php/intro"},"5238":{"id":"/docs/sdk/php/intro-0","page_id":"/docs/sdk/php/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/php/intro","content":"The experimental PHP SDK, a native extension wrapping the Rust SDK, and how to build it from source."},"5239":{"id":"/docs/sdk/php/intro-1","page_id":"/docs/sdk/php/intro","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/sdk/php/intro#requirements","content":"Requirements"},"5240":{"id":"/docs/sdk/php/intro-2","page_id":"/docs/sdk/php/intro","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/sdk/php/intro#build-and-install","content":"Build and install"},"5241":{"id":"/docs/sdk/php/intro-3","page_id":"/docs/sdk/php/intro","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/sdk/php/intro#quick-start","content":"Quick start"},"5242":{"id":"/docs/sdk/php/intro-4","page_id":"/docs/sdk/php/intro","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/sdk/php/intro#consumer-groups","content":"Consumer groups"},"5243":{"id":"/docs/sdk/php/intro-5","page_id":"/docs/sdk/php/intro","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/sdk/php/intro#api-notes","content":"API notes"},"5244":{"id":"/docs/sdk/php/intro-6","page_id":"/docs/sdk/php/intro","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/sdk/php/intro#current-status","content":"Current status"},"5245":{"id":"/docs/sdk/php/intro-7","page_id":"/docs/sdk/php/intro","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/sdk/php/intro#examples","content":"Examples"},"5246":{"id":"/docs/sdk/php/intro-8","page_id":"/docs/sdk/php/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/php/intro","content":"The Iggy PHP SDK is a native PHP extension built in Rust with ext-php-rs. It wraps the Rust SDK and exposes a synchronous Iggy\\Client: each call drives the extension's internal Tokio runtime and blocks the calling PHP thread until the operation completes. The source code is available on GitHub."},"5247":{"id":"/docs/sdk/php/intro-9","page_id":"/docs/sdk/php/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/php/intro","content":"The extension is experimental. It's defined as the apache/iggy-php composer package (php-ext type). To use it today, build it from the repository source."},"5248":{"id":"/docs/sdk/php/intro-10","page_id":"/docs/sdk/php/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/php/intro#requirements","content":"Rust and Cargo"},"5249":{"id":"/docs/sdk/php/intro-11","page_id":"/docs/sdk/php/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/php/intro#requirements","content":"PHP 8.3 or newer with php-config (non-thread-safe builds only, no ZTS)"},"5250":{"id":"/docs/sdk/php/intro-12","page_id":"/docs/sdk/php/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/php/intro#requirements","content":"cargo-php"},"5251":{"id":"/docs/sdk/php/intro-13","page_id":"/docs/sdk/php/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/php/intro#build-and-install","content":"Alternatively, skip the install and point PHP at the built library per invocation:"},"5252":{"id":"/docs/sdk/php/intro-14","page_id":"/docs/sdk/php/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/php/intro#build-and-install","content":"On macOS the library is libiggy_php.dylib."},"5253":{"id":"/docs/sdk/php/intro-15","page_id":"/docs/sdk/php/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/php/intro#quick-start","content":"The sample below expects an Iggy server on 127.0.0.1:8090:"},"5254":{"id":"/docs/sdk/php/intro-16","page_id":"/docs/sdk/php/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/php/intro#quick-start","content":"The environment variables make the server reachable through the published port (it binds to 127.0.0.1 inside the container by default) and set the iggy/iggy root credentials the sample's connection string uses."},"5255":{"id":"/docs/sdk/php/intro-17","page_id":"/docs/sdk/php/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/php/intro#quick-start","content":"Credentials in the connection string take effect on connect(). Alternatively, construct the client with a plain address (new \\Iggy\\Client('127.0.0.1:8090')) and call loginUser('iggy', 'iggy') explicitly."},"5256":{"id":"/docs/sdk/php/intro-18","page_id":"/docs/sdk/php/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/php/intro#consumer-groups","content":"consumerGroup() returns an Iggy\\Consumer that creates and joins the group by default. Consume with a bounded callback loop or a PHP iterator:"},"5257":{"id":"/docs/sdk/php/intro-19","page_id":"/docs/sdk/php/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/php/intro#consumer-groups","content":"AutoCommit::when() may queue an offset commit before the callback runs. To gate commits on the callback succeeding, use AutoCommit::disabled() and call storeOffset() after the work succeeds, as above."},"5258":{"id":"/docs/sdk/php/intro-20","page_id":"/docs/sdk/php/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/php/intro#api-notes","content":"Methods are camelCase and classes live in the Iggy namespace, for example Iggy\\Client and Iggy\\SendMessage."},"5259":{"id":"/docs/sdk/php/intro-21","page_id":"/docs/sdk/php/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/php/intro#api-notes","content":"Partition ids are 0-based: for a topic with one partition, use 0."},"5260":{"id":"/docs/sdk/php/intro-22","page_id":"/docs/sdk/php/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/php/intro#api-notes","content":"PHP strings are passed as named identifiers, including strings that contain only digits. PHP integers are passed as numeric identifiers."},"5261":{"id":"/docs/sdk/php/intro-23","page_id":"/docs/sdk/php/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/php/intro#api-notes","content":"PollingStrategy::timestamp() and timestampMicros() expect microseconds since the Unix epoch. Use PollingStrategy::timestampSeconds() for PHP time() values."},"5262":{"id":"/docs/sdk/php/intro-24","page_id":"/docs/sdk/php/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/php/intro#api-notes","content":"Large unsigned values that can overflow PHP integers, such as message checksums, are returned as decimal strings."},"5263":{"id":"/docs/sdk/php/intro-25","page_id":"/docs/sdk/php/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/php/intro#api-notes","content":"Errors are thrown as exceptions under Iggy\\Exception: AuthenticationException, ConnectionException, NotFoundException, TransientException, and the IggyException base."},"5264":{"id":"/docs/sdk/php/intro-26","page_id":"/docs/sdk/php/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/php/intro#api-notes","content":"TLS uses the Rust SDK connection-string format, for example iggy+tcp://user:pass@host:port?tls=true&tls_domain=localhost&tls_ca_file=/path/to/ca.pem."},"5265":{"id":"/docs/sdk/php/intro-27","page_id":"/docs/sdk/php/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/php/intro#api-notes","content":"The extension owns a lazy global Tokio runtime. Don't call pcntl_fork() after the first Iggy call: the child inherits file descriptors but not the runtime's worker threads."},"5266":{"id":"/docs/sdk/php/intro-28","page_id":"/docs/sdk/php/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/php/intro#current-status","content":"Iggy\\Client currently exposes stream and topic create/get/delete, message sending and polling, consumer groups with offset management, ping(), loginUser(), and raw binary requests (sendBinaryRequest()). Other administration surfaces (users, permissions, personal access tokens, partitions) aren't exposed yet."},"5267":{"id":"/docs/sdk/php/intro-29","page_id":"/docs/sdk/php/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/php/intro#examples","content":"Working examples are available in the examples/php directory. They read IGGY_CONNECTION_STRING, or build one from IGGY_HOST, IGGY_PORT, IGGY_USERNAME, and IGGY_PASSWORD:"},"5268":{"id":"/docs/sdk/python/examples","page_id":"/docs/sdk/python/examples","type":"page","content":"Examples","breadcrumbs":["Documentation","SDK","Python"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/sdk/python/examples"},"5269":{"id":"/docs/sdk/python/examples-0","page_id":"/docs/sdk/python/examples","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/python/examples","content":"Working Python examples from the core repository, covering connection strings, message headers and TLS."},"5270":{"id":"/docs/sdk/python/examples-1","page_id":"/docs/sdk/python/examples","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/sdk/python/examples#running-the-examples","content":"Running the examples"},"5271":{"id":"/docs/sdk/python/examples-2","page_id":"/docs/sdk/python/examples","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/python/examples","content":"Working examples are available in the examples/python directory. CI exercises them, so they always match the current SDK:"},"5272":{"id":"/docs/sdk/python/examples-3","page_id":"/docs/sdk/python/examples","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/python/examples","content":"getting-started - producer and consumer with optional TLS via TcpConfig"},"5273":{"id":"/docs/sdk/python/examples-4","page_id":"/docs/sdk/python/examples","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/python/examples","content":"basic - producer and consumer using connection strings"},"5274":{"id":"/docs/sdk/python/examples-5","page_id":"/docs/sdk/python/examples","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/python/examples","content":"message-headers - user headers with str, bytes, bool, int, and float values, in two variants: plain-headers (plain dict, the SDK infers wire types) and typed-headers (explicit HeaderKey/HeaderValue)"},"5275":{"id":"/docs/sdk/python/examples-6","page_id":"/docs/sdk/python/examples","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/python/examples#running-the-examples","content":"Start an Iggy server first:"},"5276":{"id":"/docs/sdk/python/examples-7","page_id":"/docs/sdk/python/examples","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/python/examples#running-the-examples","content":"The environment variables make the server reachable through the published port (it binds to 127.0.0.1 inside the container by default) and set the iggy/iggy root credentials the examples default to."},"5277":{"id":"/docs/sdk/python/examples-8","page_id":"/docs/sdk/python/examples","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/python/examples#running-the-examples","content":"Then, from examples/python, install the dependencies and run a producer/consumer pair:"},"5278":{"id":"/docs/sdk/python/examples-9","page_id":"/docs/sdk/python/examples","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/python/examples#running-the-examples","content":"For the quick-start code itself, see the Python SDK intro."},"5279":{"id":"/docs/sdk/python/intro","page_id":"/docs/sdk/python/intro","type":"page","content":"Python SDK","breadcrumbs":["Documentation","SDK","Python"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/sdk/python/intro"},"5280":{"id":"/docs/sdk/python/intro-0","page_id":"/docs/sdk/python/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/python/intro","content":"The Python SDK, a PyO3 wrapper around the Rust SDK, and how to install it."},"5281":{"id":"/docs/sdk/python/intro-1","page_id":"/docs/sdk/python/intro","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/sdk/python/intro#installation","content":"Installation"},"5282":{"id":"/docs/sdk/python/intro-2","page_id":"/docs/sdk/python/intro","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/sdk/python/intro#quick-start","content":"Quick start"},"5283":{"id":"/docs/sdk/python/intro-3","page_id":"/docs/sdk/python/intro","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/sdk/python/intro#producer","content":"Producer"},"5284":{"id":"/docs/sdk/python/intro-4","page_id":"/docs/sdk/python/intro","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/sdk/python/intro#consumer","content":"Consumer"},"5285":{"id":"/docs/sdk/python/intro-5","page_id":"/docs/sdk/python/intro","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/sdk/python/intro#beyond-the-basics","content":"Beyond the basics"},"5286":{"id":"/docs/sdk/python/intro-6","page_id":"/docs/sdk/python/intro","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/sdk/python/intro#examples","content":"Examples"},"5287":{"id":"/docs/sdk/python/intro-7","page_id":"/docs/sdk/python/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/python/intro","content":"The Iggy Python SDK is a client library that allows you to interact with the Iggy API from your Python application. It is built as a PyO3 wrapper around the Rust SDK, which means it supports TCP, QUIC, HTTP, and WebSocket transports via connection strings. The package is available on PyPI and the source code can be found on GitHub."},"5288":{"id":"/docs/sdk/python/intro-8","page_id":"/docs/sdk/python/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/python/intro","content":"Because the wheel bundles the Rust SDK, it speaks only the current Iggy wire protocol and doesn't fall back to older formats. Keep the client and server versions in step: pair the newest wheel with the newest server."},"5289":{"id":"/docs/sdk/python/intro-9","page_id":"/docs/sdk/python/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/python/intro#quick-start","content":"The samples below expect an Iggy server on 127.0.0.1:8090:"},"5290":{"id":"/docs/sdk/python/intro-10","page_id":"/docs/sdk/python/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/python/intro#quick-start","content":"The environment variables make the server reachable through the published port (it binds to 127.0.0.1 inside the container by default) and set the iggy/iggy root credentials the samples' connection string uses. Without --with-default-root-credentials (or the IGGY_ROOT_USERNAME / IGGY_ROOT_PASSWORD variables), a first boot generates a random root password instead."},"5291":{"id":"/docs/sdk/python/intro-11","page_id":"/docs/sdk/python/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/python/intro#beyond-the-basics","content":"TLS: construct the client from a TcpConfig instead of a connection string and set tls_enabled plus tls_ca_file. The getting-started example shows the full setup."},"5292":{"id":"/docs/sdk/python/intro-12","page_id":"/docs/sdk/python/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/python/intro#beyond-the-basics","content":"Consumer groups: client.consumer_group(...) returns an IggyConsumer that creates and joins the group by default and commits offsets according to the configured AutoCommit mode."},"5293":{"id":"/docs/sdk/python/intro-13","page_id":"/docs/sdk/python/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/python/intro#beyond-the-basics","content":"User headers: SendMessage(data, user_headers=...) accepts a plain dict with str, bytes, bool, int, or float values. HeaderKey/HeaderValue give explicit control over the wire type. See the message-headers examples."},"5294":{"id":"/docs/sdk/python/intro-14","page_id":"/docs/sdk/python/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/python/intro#beyond-the-basics","content":"Topic options: create_topic(..., options=...) accepts extra option keys as a dict[str, str], validated against the server's catalog. client.describe_options(\"topic\") lists the keys a server accepts."},"5295":{"id":"/docs/sdk/python/intro-15","page_id":"/docs/sdk/python/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/python/intro#beyond-the-basics","content":"Administration: user and permission management. Personal access tokens can be used for login via AutoLogin.personal_access_token(...) in the connection config. PAT management isn't exposed yet."},"5296":{"id":"/docs/sdk/python/intro-16","page_id":"/docs/sdk/python/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/python/intro#examples","content":"Working examples are available in the examples/python directory. See Examples for the list and how to run them."},"5297":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/examples","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/examples","type":"page","content":"Examples","breadcrumbs":["Documentation","SDK","Rust"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/sdk/rust/examples"},"5298":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/examples-0","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/examples","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/examples","content":"The Rust SDK examples in the core repository, and how to start a server to run them against."},"5299":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/examples-1","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/examples","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/examples","content":"In the core repository, you can find the following examples using the Rust SDK."},"5300":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/examples-2","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/examples","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/examples","content":"Before running any of them, start the server with the default root credentials. Without the flag (or IGGY_ROOT_USERNAME/IGGY_ROOT_PASSWORD) root gets a generated password and every example fails with InvalidCredentials:"},"5301":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/examples-3","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/examples","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/examples","content":"The examples are cargo example targets of the iggy_examples crate. Run them from the examples/rust directory with cargo run --example <name>. Partition IDs are 0-based: the examples send to and poll from partition 0."},"5302":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/examples-4","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/examples","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/examples","content":"Getting started - the basic example which is discussed in the getting started guide."},"5303":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/examples-5","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/examples","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/examples","content":"Basic - producer and consumer using the low-level Client trait with CLI args for transport selection (TCP/QUIC/HTTP/WebSocket)."},"5304":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/examples-6","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/examples","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/examples","content":"New SDK - an introduction to the high-level SDK as discussed in the dedicated guide."},"5305":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/examples-7","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/examples","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/examples","content":"Stream builder - examples using the IggyStream, IggyStreamProducer, and IggyStreamConsumer builder APIs as discussed in the stream builder guide."},"5306":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/examples-8","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/examples","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/examples","content":"Message envelope - the example of how to send a message with a custom envelope e.g. to differentiate between different types of messages."},"5307":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/examples-9","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/examples","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/examples","content":"Message headers - the example of how to send a message with custom headers (typed headers, message type discrimination, client-side compression)."},"5308":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/examples-10","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/examples","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/examples","content":"Multi-tenant - the larger example of how to structure your application to support multiple tenants (separated by the unique streams) with the different users, permissions, topics etc."},"5309":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/examples-11","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/examples","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/examples","content":"TCP TLS - TLS-encrypted TCP connections with custom CA certificates."},"5310":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/examples-12","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/examples","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/examples","content":"Sink data producer - generating random JSON records in bulk batches for sink connector testing."},"5311":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk","type":"page","content":"High-level SDK","breadcrumbs":["Documentation","SDK","Rust"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk"},"5312":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk-0","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk","content":"IggyProducer and IggyConsumer: batching, consumer groups and offset commits without the boilerplate."},"5313":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk-1","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk#connection-string","content":"Connection string"},"5314":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk-2","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk#producer","content":"Producer"},"5315":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk-3","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk#background-producer-configuration","content":"Background producer configuration"},"5316":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk-4","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk#send-confirmations","content":"Send confirmations"},"5317":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk-5","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk#consumer","content":"Consumer"},"5318":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk-6","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk#consumer-builder-options","content":"Consumer builder options"},"5319":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk-7","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk#auto-commit-matrix","content":"Auto-commit matrix"},"5320":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk-8","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk#raw-requests","content":"Raw requests"},"5321":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk-9","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk","content":"If you've read through the getting started guide, you might have noticed that it's quite verbose and requires a lot of boilerplate code to get started. This is where the High-level SDK comes in, as it does provide a more user-friendly interface to interact with the Iggy API for both, producer and consumer. Let's consider the following features:"},"5322":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk-10","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk","content":"Automatically creating & joining the consumer groups"},"5323":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk-11","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk","content":"Committing the offset depending on the particular mode (e.g. in the background based on some interval, after polling N messages etc.)"},"5324":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk-12","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk","content":"Batching the messages, whether it's about producing or consuming"},"5325":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk-13","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk","content":"Processing the messages as if the stream was an async iterator"},"5326":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk-14","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk","content":"Reusing the same client for both, producing and consuming on the same topic without repeating the configuration"},"5327":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk-15","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk","content":"And more..."},"5328":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk-16","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk#connection-string","content":"Instead of providing the configuration for the client, you can use the connection string. It's a string that contains all the necessary information to connect to the Iggy API, and it works with all four transports:"},"5329":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk-17","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk#connection-string","content":"Which can be used to create the client like this:"},"5330":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk-18","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk#connection-string","content":"Options are appended as query parameters. For TCP:"},"5331":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk-19","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk#connection-string","content":"The option keys differ per transport (for example QUIC uses reconnection_max_retries and reconnection_reestablish_after where TCP uses reconnection_retries and reestablish_after). Unknown keys are rejected as hard errors. See Connection Strings for the full per-transport option tables and defaults."},"5332":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk-20","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk#connection-string","content":"Unless you need to provide a specific implementation of the client-side Encryptor, Partitioner or adjust some other settings, you should be good to go with the connection string. On the other hand, you can always make use of IggyClientBuilder::from_connection_string() to extend the options on top of the provided connection string."},"5333":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk-21","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk#connection-string","content":"The IggyClient internally wraps its transport in an IggyRwLock, so a single instance is cheap to clone and can be shared across multiple tasks. Typically, it's a good idea to create a separate connection for producing and consuming, but it's not a requirement."},"5334":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk-22","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk#producer","content":"The producer is a high-level abstraction that allows you to send messages to the topic. It's quite simple to use and doesn't require you to handle the offsets, partitions or any other low-level details. To begin with, you can simply invoke client.producer() to get the IggyProducerBuilder allowing you to configure the producer. Let's take a look at the basic example:"},"5335":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk-23","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk#producer","content":"The code above will result in creating the producer that will try to send the messages in batches of 1000 every 5 milliseconds. You can choose between the direct (an instant producer) or the background (which will send the messages in the background by buffering them). The partitioning is set to balanced which means that the producer will try to distribute the messages evenly across all the partitions. The init() method is used to ensure that the producer is ready to send the messages by validating the existence of the stream, topic etc."},"5336":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk-24","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk#producer","content":"Finally, you can use the send() method to send the messages to the topic. The producer never needs to be a mutable binding: both init() and send() take &self, so a plain let producer is enough for its whole life. Here's how you can send the messages:"},"5337":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk-25","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk#producer","content":"Of course, you can provide the message headers, custom binary serialization etc. as it's the same IggyMessage type as the one used in the low-level API (use IggyMessage::builder() to set headers or a custom ID). The producer will take care of the rest, including the retries, partitioning, batching etc."},"5338":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk-26","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk#producer","content":"Besides send(), the producer also offers send_one(message), send_with_partitioning(messages, partitioning) to override the partitioning per call, and send_to(stream, topic, messages, partitioning) to target a different stream and topic."},"5339":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk-27","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk#background-producer-configuration","content":"The background mode buffers messages and sends them from shard workers, 