mp, and the recomputed batch_checksum are final); each backup re-derives the expected stamp from its own position and refuses a mismatch. Replication is chain-form: the primary sends to (replica + 1) % replica_count, each backup forwards to its own successor, and the chain stops when the next hop is the primary. RepairPrepare (19) is byte-identical to a journaled prepare with only the command byte rewritten."],[4137,"For multi-instance deployment patterns (Docker, Kubernetes, fan-out topologies), connection pool tuning, and full performance analysis, see the upstream http_sink README."],[2213,"For our purpose, we will focus on the basic scenario in order to keep things simple. Before we begin implementing the consumer and producer apps, we need to start the Iggy streaming server."],[3375,"For production deployments, provide proper certificates via cert_file and key_file. Ephemeral certificates change on every start and cannot be verified by clients. See Networking for the surrounding transport configuration."],[4118,"For production throughput with JSON-representable payloads, prefer ndjson or json_array, which collapse N round trips per poll cycle into one."],[2209,"For real-world applications, you might typically prefer the high-level SDK, which provides IggyProducer and IggyConsumer builders with auto-batching, consumer groups, offset management, reconnection, and retry logic out of the box. Check the Stream Builder docs for the most ergonomic API."],[1424,"For SDKs without redirection coverage, connect clients to the current primary for write-heavy workloads, or front the cluster's HTTP transport with a load balancer and rely on follower HTTP forwarding for control-plane operations."],[5133,"For the code walkthrough, see the quick start on the intro page, which follows the same example."],[4145,"For the complete list and implementation context, see the upstream http_sink README."],[5455,"For the full getting started tutorial, see Getting Started. For the high-level producer/consumer builders, see High-level SDK and Stream Builder."],[5210,"For the quick-start code itself, see the Node.js SDK intro."],[5278,"For the quick-start code itself, see the Python SDK intro."],[1284,"Force a flush of the unsaved buffer of a partition to disk. With -f, --fsync the data is also fsynced."],[2268,"Format"],[4439,"Format"],[3740,"Format of payload_column: bytea (alias raw), text, or json_direct (alias jsonb)"],[2489,"Format:"],[297,"Forward a logout"],[289,"Forward a verified login"],[4003,"Forwarded as the columns Stream Load header; pins column order for CSV"],[295,"ForwardLogout"],[416,"ForwardLogout (28): client 128, nonce 144, session 160, request 168."],[299,"ForwardLogoutResult"],[417,"ForwardLogoutResult (29): nonce 128, client 144, commit 160, outcome at 255: 0 Ok, 1 NotPrimary, 2 PipelineFull, 3 InProgress, 4 Canceled."],[287,"ForwardRegister"],[414,"ForwardRegister (26): client 128, nonce 144, user_id 160."],[291,"ForwardRegisterResult"],[415,"ForwardRegisterResult (27): nonce 128, client 144, epoch 160, watermark 168, outcome byte at 255: 0 Ok, 1 NotPrimary, 2 NotCaughtUp, 3 PipelineFull, 4 InProgress, 5 Canceled, 6 ClientIdOwnedByAnotherUser. epoch and watermark must be zero on any non-Ok outcome."],[171,"Frame integrity"],[304,"Frame seal (checksum, bytes 0..16): XxHash3-64 over header bytes 16..256, widened to u128. Sealed on every replica message except Prepare, RepairPrepare, and the three handshake frames (whose integrity comes from the keyed MAC). Verified once, at typed decode, before any field validation; a mismatch drops the frame."],[674,"Frame seal. Not used on client-facing frames; send zero."],[187,"Frames are the standard [256-byte header][optional body] with size at offset 48. The maximum frame is 64 MiB by default (message_bus.max_message_size). Headers are #[repr(C)], decoded zero-copy, and require 16-byte alignment."],[633,"Framing"],[1106,"Framing: the 256-byte headers every client-facing message rides in."