le and is the only shard that commits metadata mutations, while every shard holds a read handle. This provides:"],[1559,"shared_secret = new alone, closing the window"],[1558,"shared_secret = new, previous_shared_secret = old on every node"],[1557,"shared_secret = old, previous_shared_secret = new on every node"],[1329,"Shell completions"],[1306,"Show info about the current connection: client ID, user ID, server address, protocol."],[1307,"Show server statistics: streams, topics, partitions, messages, plus host OS details. -o, --output <table|list|json|toml> selects the format (default: table)."],[3010,"shutdown_drain_timeout"],[3016,"shutdown_join_timeout"],[3013,"shutdown_poll_interval"],[2702,"Signing algorithm for self-issued tokens."],[2726,"Signing key. Empty means a secure random secret is generated on each server start."],[3379,"Signing secrets: encoding_secret and decoding_secret default to empty, which makes the server generate a secure random secret on every start. That's a safe single-node default with two consequences: issued tokens die on restart, and in a cluster each node signs with its own key, so bearers are node-local and follower-to-primary request forwarding stays disabled. For clusters, configure an identical secret on every node (prefer the IGGY_HTTP_JWT_ENCODING_SECRET/IGGY_HTTP_JWT_DECODING_SECRET environment variables over on-disk config), or enable cluster.auth so the JWT key derives from the cluster's shared PSK."],[5487,"Similar to the previous StreamBuilder, the IggyConsumerMessageExt is used to process messages using\nthe PrintEventConsumer implementation of the MessageConsumer trait."],[1607,"Simulator"],[2238,"Since our IggyClient implements the common Client trait, you can find lots of the different methods to interact with the server, also from the administrative point of view, e.g. creating the streams, topics etc."],[2016,"Single binary deployment (no external dependencies)"],[26,"Single binary, Docker & Helm, NUMA-aware CPU affinity"],[4157,"Single destination table"],[3519,"Single-node durability is a per-topic decision. For the strongest guarantee, create the topic with enforce_fsync=true. Every write is then fsynced before it is acknowledged. Without it, the flush thresholds (messages_required_to_save, size_of_messages_required_to_save) bound how much buffered data a crash can lose, at much higher throughput."],[1650,"Sink"],[1666,"Sink"],[4526,"Sink"],[1855,"Sink connector config (connectors/protobuf_sink.toml):"],[4537,"Sink connector config (connectors/stdout.toml):"],[4532,"Sink connectors are responsible for writing data from Iggy streams to external systems or destinations. They provide a way to integrate Apache Iggy with various data sources and destinations, enabling data flow between Iggy and external systems."],[5310,"Sink data producer - generating random JSON records in bulk batches for sink connector testing."],[5205,"sink-data-producer - bulk data generation for sink connectors"],[5169,"sinkdataproducer - high-volume data generation (1000+ messages per batch) with realistic records, for testing and benchmarking."],[1698,"Sinks are responsible for consuming the messages from the configured stream(s) and topic(s) and sending them further to the specified destination. For example, the Quickwit sink connector is responsible for sending the messages to the Quickwit indexer."],[687,"size"],[882,"size"],[998,"size"],[2950,"size"],[4499,"size"],[311,"Size"],[326,"Size"],[666,"Size"],[861,"Size"],[977,"Size"],[1119,"Size"],[1164,"Size"],[2905,"Size at which a log file rotates. 0 means one unbounded file, which disables size-based rotation."],[4500,"size or messages"],[3495,"size_of_messages_required_to_save"],[3428,"size_of_messages_required_to_save (default 1 MiB): byte threshold."],[2645,"size_of_messages_required_to_save topic option"],[3445,"Size-based retention (max_topic_size): the cluster has no single owner of a topic-wide total, so each partition enforces an equal share: max_topic_size / partition_count, counted over sealed bytes. The share is floored at one sealed-segment ceiling (segment_size plus the maximum message-bus frame, since a sealed segment can overshoot by one batch). A cap that divides below that floor is raised to it, so the policy always means at least \"keep the newest sealed segment\". Once sealed bytes exceed the budget, the oldest sealed segments are trimmed. There is no percentage-based early trigger."],[3040,"Slots in the VSR client table: how many distinct clients (TCP/QUIC/WebSocket virtual clients and HTTP sessions together) hold live session state. When full, the client with the oldest last commit is evicted and re-registers on its next request. The HTTP session cap tracks this at half. Must be between 2 and 65536."],[3725,"snake_case_columns"],[76,"snapshot"],[1228,"snapshot"],[400,"snapshot.bin verbatim (MessagePack, snapshot format version 3); frontier = sequence number"],[3450,"snapshot.bin: a MessagePack (rmp-serde) snapshot of the whole metadata state machine, written at checkpoints so the journal can be truncated. Recovery loads the snapshot, then replays journal entries newer than it, rebuilding the client session table alongside."],[4708,"Snapshots"],[2237,"So far, so good, however, before we will be able to publish any messages to our streaming server, at first, we need to create the stream, topic and partition(s) - if you're unfamiliar with these concepts, please refer to concepts where all of them are described in-depth."],[3487,"Soft size limit per segment: a segment may close one whole batch past it."],[5092,"Some operations are TCP-only and throw FeatureUnavailableException on HTTP: joining/leaving a consumer group, GetMeAsync, DeleteSegmentsAsync, and SendBinaryRequestAsync."],[3552,"Some pages visible in the navigation (Clients, Logs, terminal, server settings) are placeholders that aren't functional yet. See the roadmap for what is planned."],[1596,"Source"],[1651,"Source"],[1664,"Source"],[3817,"Source"],[4153,"Source Compatibility"],[1835,"Source Configuration"],[1854,"Source connector config (connectors/protobuf_source.toml):"],[3828,"Source connector config (connectors/random.toml):"],[3823,"Source connectors are responsible for ingesting data from external sources into Apache Iggy. They provide a way to integrate Apache Iggy with various data sources, such as databases, message queues, or file systems."],[1735,"Source connectors can persist their position (see the SDK documentation) into the directory configured in the [state] section:"],[1847,"Source connectors can persist their position between restarts. Each batch of ProducedMessages may carry an optional ConnectorState - the runtime saves it to a file after the batch is sent to Iggy, and passes the last saved state to new() on the next startup. ConnectorState wraps raw bytes and provides serialize/deserialize helpers backed by MessagePack. Sinks don't manage state. Their offsets are tracked by Iggy consumer groups. See the Observability page for where and how state files are stored."],[1862,"source_format / target_format: Formats to convert between - any schema value (json, raw, text, proto, flat_buffer, avro)"],[1701,"Sources are responsible for producing the messages to the configured stream(s) and topic(s). For example, the Random source connector generates random messages that are then sent to the configured stream and topic."],[1314,"Space-separated subset of filesystem_overview, process_list, resource_usage, test, server_logs, server_config, all"],[1510,"Spanning multiple hosts"],[1090,"StaleClient"],[2050,"Standalone - as a separate container via docker pull apache/iggy-web-ui"],[3526,"Standalone mode"],[3179,"Standalone Web UI"],[2524,"Start after a specific timestamp"],[5206,"Start an Iggy server first:"],[5275,"Start an Iggy server first:"],[567,"Start at this offset"],[570,"Start at this timestamp (microseconds)"],[1527,"Start each replica in a separate terminal. Use a different data path for every process:"],[2522,"Start from a specific offset"],[573,"Start from the beginning (value ignored)"],[2526,"Start from the earliest available message"],[576,"Start from the end (value ignored)"],[1266,"Start from the first message in the partition"],[1264,"Start from the given offset"],[1268,"Start from the last message"],[2528,"Start from the latest message"],[1693,"Start the connector runtime cargo run --bin iggy-connectors -r - you should be able to browse Quickwit UI with records being constantly added to the events index. At the same time, you should see the new messages being added to the example_stream stream and example_topic topic by the Random source connector - you can use Iggy Web UI to browse the data. The messages will have applied the basic fields transformations."],[1691,"Start the Iggy server and invoke the following commands via Iggy CLI to create the example streams and topics used by the sample connectors."],[2264,"Start the Iggy server, and then producer and consumer applications respectively - you should observe your messages being streamed flawlessly :)"],[5131,"Start the server with TLS enabled, using the development certificates from the repository:"],[3652,"Starting cursor timestamp on first run"],[2203,"Starting the Iggy server"],[5117,"Starting the server"],[5158,"Starting the server"],[3720,"Starting value for the tracking column"],[231,"StartView"],[378,"StartView (12): op 128, commit 136 (max over the DVC quorum), group 144, incarnation 240 (echo of a RequestStartView incarnation, zero when unsolicited). Body = the view's suffix as packed PrepareHeaders, highest op first; empty is legal. Unicast when answering a probe, broadcast otherwise."],[223,"StartViewChange"],[376,"StartViewChange (10): header-only, just group at 128. Broadcast when a replica suspects the primary."],[1829,"State"],[3559,"State"],[1712,"State files"],[3597,"State is managed by the connectors runtime, not by the connector itself. Along with each batch of produced messages, the connector returns its progress (last poll timestamp plus document, poll, and error counters). The runtime persists it atomically to a per-connector file, source_<key>.state, under the [state] path from the runtime config (default local_state). On restart the runtime hands the saved state back to the connector, which resumes polling from the last processed timestamp."],[1660,"State persistence is file-based (using MessagePack serialization) and applies to source connectors only, tracking the last polled position."],[3598,"State persistence requires no plugin configuration. To reset the connector's position, stop the runtime and delete the state file."],[179,"State transfer"],[283,"StateChunk"],[389,"StateChunk (25): nonce 128, offset 144, group 152, artifact 160. Body = raw artifact bytes. Chunks carry no per-chunk integrity; each artifact is verified whole, by length and XxHash3-64, against its manifest entry."],[275,"StateTransferTarget"],[387,"StateTransferTarget (23): nonce 128, commit_op 144, group 152, available 160, unavailable_transient 161, commit_max 168. available = 0 is a header-only refusal. Body = the state manifest."],[1683,"Statically Typed: Ensures type safety and compile-time checks, reducing runtime errors."],[1227,"stats"],[957,"status"],[1578,"Status"],[322,"Statuses: 0 Ok, 1 UnknownCommand, 2 ClusterMismatch, 3 DirectionalRule; values 4 and 5 are reserved (never sent), and a dialer treats any unknown status byte as a rejection. The MAC key derives from cluster.auth.shared_secret (32+ bytes; previous_shared_secret gives a rotation window), and with cluster TLS enabled (TLS 1.3 only, ALPN iggy-replica) the TLS exporter is folded into every MAC as channel binding. The handshake authenticates cluster membership, not per-replica identity: one shared secret for the whole cluster."],[4562,"Stdout Sink"],[3806,"Stop producing after this many messages in total; omit for unlimited"],[3383,"Storage Engine"],[2081,"Storage structure"],[2890,"Storage sub-paths chain relative to each other: [system.runtime] path = \"runtime\" (runtime data, including current_config.toml), [system.logging] path = \"logs\", [system.stream] path = \"streams\", [system.topic] path = \"topics\" (relative to the stream path), [system.partition] path = \"partitions\" (relative to the topic path)."],[3942,"storage_backend_type"],[135,"Store consumer offset"],[480,"Store consumer offset. Code: 121."],[4370,"Store messages from Iggy topics in a PostgreSQL table, with optional table creation and configurable payload storage."],[4635,"Store stream/topic/partition/offset/timestamp/schema fields"],[4167,"store_access_key_id: The access key ID of the object storage."],[4170,"store_class: The storage class to use. Currently, only S3-compatible storage is supported."],[134,"store_consumer_offset"],[4169,"store_region: The region of the object storage. Required. For S3-compatible stores that ignore it, supply any placeholder value."],[4168,"store_secret_access_key: The secret key used to upload data to the object storage."],[4166,"store_url: The URL of the object storage for data uploads."],[844,"StoreConsumerOffset"],[4579,"Stored Shape"],[1312,"stored, deflated, bzip2, zstd, lzma, xz"],[5068,"StoreOffsetAsync(offset, partitionId, resetLastPolled = false) stores the offset for a partition; pass resetLastPolled: true to also move the consumer's cached last-polled position so the next poll resumes past the stored offset. DeleteOffsetAsync(partitionId) clears the stored offset."],[2546,"Storing and comparing results"],[2540,"Strategies resolve in the client on binary transports, and on the server for the HTTP API and the Node SDK."],[1261,"Strategy"],[2519,"Strategy"],[2532,"Strategy"],[2259,"strategy - the way in which we want to poll the messages. The default one being Offset (underlying PollingKind enum) means that we will start polling the messages from the particular offset provided in the value field - it's on the client, to keep track of the most recent offset. On the other hand, we could also use the different kind, for example Next, which means, that the next messages will be returned to the client, depending on the so-called consumer offset value stored on the server side. The client may not need to track the offset on its own anymore, but instead, call store_offset() to save it on the server (e.g. after each processed message or the whole batch), or make use of  auto_commit: true, to automatically commit the offset on the server side, once the messages are fetched (this one results in the so called at-most-once delivery mode)."],[471,"strategy is a polling strategy. count is the requested number of messages. auto_commit = 1 stores the consumer offset server-side as part of the poll."],[1208,"stream"],[2127,"Stream"],[5456,"Stream Builder"],[5305,"Stream builder - examples using the IggyStream, IggyStreamProducer, and IggyStreamConsumer builder APIs as discussed in the stream builder guide."],[3319,"Stream permissions - scoped to one stream and all its topics"],[3673,"Stream rows from PostgreSQL into Iggy topics by incremental table polling or change data capture over logical replication."],[3351,"Stream scope: manage_stream, read_stream, manage_topics, read_topics, poll_messages, send_messages, plus an optional per-topic map. read_stream includes read_topics and poll_messages within that stream."],[2255,"stream_id - the ID of the stream (numeric or string) from which we want to poll the messages."],[2807,"stream_receive_window"],[3263,"stream_receive_window is the per-stream slice of receive_window, equal to it because there is a single stream. Lower it if you ever raise the stream cap."],[5204,"stream-builder - end-to-end walkthrough: create a stream and topic, produce, and consume"],[3355,"Stream-scoped poll_messages on stream 1 lets the user poll from all topics in stream 1, but not from other streams."],[3400,"Stream, topic, and partition ids are numeric and 0-based. Each pa